perseverare'' (to persist) ← ''per-'' (thoroughly) + ''severus'' (strict, serious)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"per-","root":"sever","suffix":"-ance"},"relatedWords":["persevere","persistent","persistence"],"isEssential":true,"partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"/ˌpɜːrsəˈvɪrəns/","contextSentence":"Grit is passion and perseverance for very long-term goals.","segmentIndex":39},{"word":"stamina","definition":"the ability to sustain prolonged physical or mental effort","koreanMeaning":"체력, 지구력; 장시간 노력을 지속하는 능력","etymology":"Latin ''stamina'' (threads of life spun by the Fates) ← plural of ''stamen'' (thread, fiber)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"stamen","suffix":"-a"},"relatedWords":["endurance","resilience","vigor"],"isEssential":true,"partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"/ˈstæmɪnə/","contextSentence":"Grit is having stamina.","segmentIndex":40},{"word":"stratospheric","definition":"extremely high; reaching the stratosphere (figuratively: exceptionally great)","koreanMeaning":"엄청나게 높은, 천문학적인","etymology":"Latin ''stratum'' (layer) + Greek ''sphaira'' (sphere) + ''-ic'' → relating to the upper atmosphere → metaphor for extremely high","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"strato-","root":"spher","suffix":"-ic"},"relatedWords":["stratosphere","atmospheric","astronomical"],"isEssential":false,"partOfSpeech":"adjective","phonetic":"/ˌstrætəˈsfɛrɪk/","contextSentence":"Some of my strongest performers did not have stratospheric IQ scores.","segmentIndex":8},{"word":"parallelogram","definition":"a four-sided shape with opposite sides parallel and equal in length","koreanMeaning":"평행사변형","etymology":"Greek ''parallēlogrammon'' ← ''parallēlos'' (parallel) + ''grammē'' (line)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"parallel-","root":"gram","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["parallel","rectangle","rhombus"],"isEssential":false,"partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"/ˌpærəˈlɛləˌɡræm/","contextSentence":"Ratios, decimals, the area of a parallelogram.","segmentIndex":11},{"word":"predictor","definition":"a thing that predicts; a factor indicating a future outcome","koreanMeaning":"예측 인자, 예측 변수","etymology":"Latin ''praedicere'' (to foretell) ← ''prae-'' (before) + ''dicere'' (to say)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"pre-","root":"dict","suffix":"-or"},"relatedWords":["predict","prediction","predictable"],"isEssential":true,"partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"/prɪˈdɪktər/","contextSentence":"One characteristic emerged as a significant predictor of success.","segmentIndex":37},{"word":"cadet","definition":"a young trainee in the armed services or police force","koreanMeaning":"사관생도, 훈련생","etymology":"French ''cadet'' (younger son) ← Gascon ''capdet'' (captain, chief) ← Latin ''caput'' (head)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"cadet","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["cadre","captain","chief"],"isEssential":false,"partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"/kəˈdɛt/","contextSentence":"We tried to predict which cadets would stay in military training and which would drop out.","segmentIndex":28},{"word":"inversely","definition":"in an opposite manner; in reverse proportion","koreanMeaning":"반대로, 반비례하여","etymology":"Latin ''inversus'' (turned upside down) ← ''in-'' (into) + ''vertere'' (to turn)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"in-","root":"vers","suffix":"-ly"},"relatedWords":["inverse","invert","reverse","convert"],"isEssential":true,"partOfSpeech":"adverb","phonetic":"/ɪnˈvɜːrsli/","contextSentence":"Grit is usually unrelated or even inversely related to measures of talent.","segmentIndex":66},{"word":"questionnaire","definition":"a set of printed questions used for gathering information from respondents","koreanMeaning":"설문지, 질문서","etymology":"French ''questionnaire'' ← ''questionner'' (to question) ← Latin ''quaestio'' (inquiry)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"question","suffix":"-naire"},"relatedWords":["question","query","inquiry","survey"],"isEssential":false,"partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"/ˌkwɛstʃəˈnɛr/","contextSentence":"I asked thousands of high school juniors to take grit questionnaires.","segmentIndex":47},{"word":"intuition","definition":"the ability to understand something immediately, without conscious reasoning","koreanMeaning":"직관, 직감","etymology":"Latin ''intuitio'' (a looking at) ← ''intueri'' (to look at, consider) ← ''in-'' (at) + ''tueri'' (to look)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"in-","root":"tuit","suffix":"-ion"},"relatedWords":["intuitive","intuit","tuition"],"isEssential":true,"partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"/ˌɪntjuˈɪʃən/","contextSentence":"We need to take our best ideas, our strongest intuitions, and we need to test them.","segmentIndex":79},{"word":"consulting","definition":"the business of giving expert advice to people working in a professional field","koreanMeaning":"컨설팅, 자문; 전문 상담 서비스","etymology":"Latin ''consultare'' (to deliberate, take counsel) ← ''consulere'' (to take counsel)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"con-","root":"sult","suffix":"-ing"},"relatedWords":["consultant","consult","consultation"],"isEssential":false,"partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"/kənˈsʌltɪŋ/","contextSentence":"I left a very demanding job in management consulting.","segmentIndex":1},{"word":"rookie","definition":"a person new to a job, activity, or organization; a beginner","koreanMeaning":"신입, 초보자, 루키","etymology":"possibly alteration of ''recruit''; origin uncertain, first recorded in American English 1890s","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"rookie","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["novice","beginner","newcomer"],"isEssential":false,"partOfSpeech":"noun/adjective","phonetic":"/ˈrʊki/","contextSentence":"We studied rookie teachers working in really tough neighborhoods.","segmentIndex":31},{"word":"commitment","definition":"a pledge or obligation; dedication to a cause or activity","koreanMeaning":"헌신, 약속, 전념","etymology":"Latin ''committere'' (to connect, entrust) ← ''com-'' (together) + ''mittere'' (to send)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"com-","root":"mit","suffix":"-ment"},"relatedWords":["commit","committee","commission"],"isEssential":true,"partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"/kəˈmɪtmənt/","contextSentence":"There are many talented individuals who simply do not follow through on their commitments.","segmentIndex":64},{"word":"mindset","definition":"the established set of attitudes held by someone; a way of thinking","koreanMeaning":"사고방식, 마인드셋","etymology":"''mind'' (Old English ''gemynd'', memory) + ''set'' (Old English ''settan'', to place) — compound coined 1930s","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"mind+set","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["mindful","mind-frame","attitude","outlook"],"isEssential":true,"partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"/ˈmaɪndˌsɛt/","contextSentence":"The best idea I''ve heard about building grit in kids is something called growth mindset.","segmentIndex":68},{"word":"significantly","definition":"in a way that is large or important enough to have an effect or be noticed","koreanMeaning":"상당히, 유의미하게, 현저하게","etymology":"Latin ''significare'' (to signify) ← ''signum'' (sign) + ''facere'' (to make) + ''-ly''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"sign-","root":"fic","suffix":"-antly"},"relatedWords":["significant","significance","signify"],"isEssential":true,"partOfSpeech":"adverb","phonetic":"/sɪɡˈnɪfɪkəntli/","contextSentence":"Grittier kids were significantly more likely to graduate.","segmentIndex":49}]'::jsonb, '[{"pattern":"What struck me was that...","example":"What struck me was that IQ was not the only difference between my best and my worst students.","explanationKo":"강조 구문 (Cleft sentence). ''What + 동사 + me + was + that절''로 특정 사실을 강조합니다. What이 이끄는 명사절이 주어, that절이 보어 역할.","segmentIndex":6},{"pattern":"What if + 주어 + 동사...?","example":"But what if doing well in school and in life depends on much more than your ability to learn quickly and easily?","explanationKo":"가정/제안의 의문문. ''What if...?''로 가능성을 제기하며 청중의 사고를 유도합니다. TED 강연에서 주제를 소개할 때 자주 사용되는 수사적 질문.","segmentIndex":21},{"pattern":"It wasn''t X. It wasn''t Y. It was Z.","example":"And it wasn''t social intelligence. It wasn''t good looks, physical health, and it wasn''t IQ. It was grit.","explanationKo":"배제 후 공개 패턴 (Elimination reveal). ''It wasn''t...''을 반복하여 오답을 배제한 후 핵심 답을 극적으로 공개합니다. 발표에서 긴장감 조성용.","segmentIndex":38},{"pattern":"Grit is -ing (정의 나열)","example":"Grit is having stamina. Grit is sticking with your future, day in, day out.","explanationKo":"동명사(-ing)를 사용한 정의 나열. 추상적 개념을 구체적 행동으로 풀어서 설명할 때 ''X is -ing...''을 반복합니다. 병렬 구조(parallelism)의 대표적 활용.","segmentIndex":40},{"pattern":"Turns out (that)...","example":"Turns out that grittier kids were significantly more likely to graduate.","explanationKo":"구어체 결과 보고. ''It turns out that...''의 축약형으로, 연구 결과나 의외의 사실을 소개할 때 사용합니다. 학술 발표에서 자연스러움을 더하는 표현.","segmentIndex":49},{"pattern":"Even when I matched them on...","example":"Even when I matched them on every characteristic I could measure, things like family income, standardized achievement test scores.","explanationKo":"''even when''으로 양보절을 이끌어, 결과가 변수를 통제한 후에도 유효함을 강조합니다. 연구 발표에서 결과의 신뢰성을 높이는 학술적 표현.","segmentIndex":50},{"pattern":"The honest answer is...","example":"The honest answer is, I don''t know.","explanationKo":"솔직한 고백 패턴. 청중의 기대를 인정한 뒤 정직하게 한계를 시인합니다. 신뢰를 구축하는 수사 전략으로, TED 강연에서 매우 효과적.","segmentIndex":61},{"pattern":"So far, the best idea I''ve heard about...","example":"So far, the best idea I''ve heard about building grit in kids is something called growth mindset.","explanationKo":"''So far''(지금까지)로 시간적 한정을 두고, 현재완료 ''I''ve heard''로 경험을 기반으로 한 제안을 합니다. 겸손하면서도 권위 있는 추천 표현.","segmentIndex":67},{"pattern":"In other words, we need to be X about Y-ing.","example":"In other words, we need to be gritty about getting our kids grittier.","explanationKo":"핵심 메시지 요약. ''In other words''로 바꿔 말하기를 시작하며, 강연의 핵심을 한 문장으로 압축합니다. 재귀적 표현(그릿에 대해 그릿을 가져야 한다)으로 여운.","segmentIndex":83},{"pattern":"not just X, not just Y, but for Z","example":"Day in, day out, not just for the week, not just for the month, but for years.","explanationKo":"점진적 확대 (Climax/Gradation). ''not just...not just...but''로 시간 범위를 점점 넓혀가며 강조합니다. 청중의 기대를 단계적으로 확장하는 수사법.","segmentIndex":41}]'::jsonb, '[{"original":"left a","spoken":"/lɛf.tə/","type":"linking","koreanPhonetic":"레프터","practiceGuide":"''left''의 /t/가 ''a''의 모음에 연결되어 한 덩어리로 발음됩니다.","start":15.14,"end":15.46,"segmentIndex":1},{"original":"struck me","spoken":"/strʌk.mi/","type":"linking","koreanPhonetic":"스트럭 미","practiceGuide":"''struck''의 /k/가 ''me''의 /m/에 바로 연결됩니다. 자음+자음 연결.","start":36.3,"end":37.0,"segmentIndex":6},{"original":"best and","spoken":"/bɛs.tən/","type":"linking","koreanPhonetic":"베스튼","practiceGuide":"''best''의 /t/가 ''and''의 /æ/에 연결되고, ''and''가 /ən/으로 약화됩니다.","start":41.3,"end":42.0,"segmentIndex":7},{"original":"got me","spoken":"/ɡɑːʔ.mi/","type":"elision","koreanPhonetic":"가''미","practiceGuide":"''got''의 /t/가 성문파열음(glottal stop)으로 대체됩니다. 미국 영어 구어체에서 매우 흔한 현상.","start":54.0,"end":54.8,"segmentIndex":10},{"original":"kinds of","spoken":"/kaɪnzəv/","type":"reduction","koreanPhonetic":"카인저브","practiceGuide":"''of''가 /əv/로 약화되어 앞 단어에 붙어 발음됩니다. ''kind of''는 일상에서 /kaɪndə/로도 축약.","start":55.0,"end":55.8,"segmentIndex":10},{"original":"worked hard and long enough","spoken":"/wɜːrkt.hɑːr.dən.lɔːŋ.ɪˈnʌf/","type":"linking","koreanPhonetic":"워크트 하든 롱이너프","practiceGuide":"''hard''의 /d/가 ''and''와 연결, ''and''가 /ən/으로 약화. ''long enough''는 /ŋ/+/ɪ/ 연음.","start":71.5,"end":75.5,"segmentIndex":14},{"original":"went to","spoken":"/wɛn.tə/","type":"reduction","koreanPhonetic":"웬터","practiceGuide":"''to''가 약형 /tə/로 발음됩니다. 기능어(전치사, 관사 등)는 대부분 약형으로 발음.","start":108.0,"end":108.6,"segmentIndex":23},{"original":"start over again","spoken":"/stɑːr.toʊ.vər.əˈɡɛn/","type":"linking","koreanPhonetic":"스타르토우버어겐","practiceGuide":"''start''의 /t/가 ''over''의 /oʊ/에 연결. ''over again''도 /r/+/ə/ 연음으로 이어집니다.","start":352.0,"end":355.0,"segmentIndex":82},{"original":"it wasn''t","spoken":"/ɪt.ˈwʌzn̩t/","type":"reduction","koreanPhonetic":"잇 워즌트","practiceGuide":"축약형 ''wasn''t''에서 /t/가 거의 안 들림. 반복 사용으로 리듬감 형성.","start":172.0,"end":173.0,"segmentIndex":38},{"original":"grit is","spoken":"/ɡrɪ.tɪz/","type":"linking","koreanPhonetic":"그리리즈","practiceGuide":"''grit''의 /t/가 ''is''의 /ɪ/에 연결되어 flap /ɾ/로 발음됩니다. 미국 영어의 flapping 현상.","start":186.7,"end":187.5,"segmentIndex":40},{"original":"day in, day out","spoken":"/deɪ.ɪn deɪ.aʊt/","type":"linking","koreanPhonetic":"데이인 데이아웃","practiceGuide":"관용 표현. ''day''의 /eɪ/가 ''in''의 /ɪ/에 자연스럽게 이어집니다. 강세 리듬이 핵심.","start":191.5,"end":193.0,"segmentIndex":41},{"original":"follow through on","spoken":"/ˈfɑː.loʊ.θruː.ɑːn/","type":"linking","koreanPhonetic":"팔로우 쓰루온","practiceGuide":"구동사 ''follow through on''. ''through''의 /uː/가 ''on''의 /ɑː/에 연결됩니다.","start":280.0,"end":283.1,"segmentIndex":64}]'::jsonb, '{"sections":[{"section":"Personal Story: IQ Isn''t Everything","startSegment":0,"endSegment":9,"summary":"Opening: Personal story about teaching 7th grade math and discovering IQ isn''t everything","keyPoints":["교사로서의 경험을 통해 IQ가 성적의 유일한 요인이 아님을 발견","성적 우수자가 반드시 IQ가 높지 않았고, IQ가 높아도 성적이 낮은 학생 존재"],"signalExpressions":["When I was 27 years old","What struck me was that"]},{"section":"From Classroom to Graduate School","startSegment":10,"endSegment":22,"summary":"Transition: From classroom observation to graduate school — reframing the question from IQ to motivation","keyPoints":["7학년 수학은 어렵지만 불가능하지 않음 — 노력의 문제","교육에는 동기 부여/심리학적 관점의 이해가 필요","핵심 질문: ''학습 속도 이상의 것이 성공을 결정한다면?''"],"signalExpressions":["And that got me thinking","I came to the conclusion that","But what if"]},{"section":"Research Across Extreme Environments","startSegment":23,"endSegment":38,"summary":"Research journey: Studies across West Point, Spelling Bee, schools, and companies — all pointing to one factor","keyPoints":["다양한 극한 환경에서 성공 예측 인자 연구","웨스트포인트, 철자 맞히기 대회, 초임 교사, 영업사원 대상","사회적 지능, 외모, 건강, IQ 모두 아닌 하나의 특성 발견"],"signalExpressions":["I started studying","My research team and I","In all those very different contexts"]},{"section":"Defining Grit: Passion and Perseverance","startSegment":39,"endSegment":54,"summary":"Core concept: Defining grit and presenting evidence from Chicago public schools","keyPoints":["그릿 = 장기 목표에 대한 열정 + 인내","마라톤 비유: 인생은 단거리가 아닌 마라톤","시카고 연구: 그릿 높은 학생이 졸업률 유의미하게 높음","가정 소득, 시험 점수 등 변수 통제 후에도 유효"],"signalExpressions":["It was grit","Grit is","Turns out that"]},{"section":"The Gap: How to Build Grit","startSegment":55,"endSegment":75,"summary":"The gap: We know little about building grit — growth mindset as the best lead so far","keyPoints":["그릿을 키우는 방법에 대한 과학적 지식 부족 고백","재능 ≠ 그릿 (오히려 반비례)","캐럴 드웩의 성장 마인드셋이 현재 최선의 아이디어","뇌가 변화할 수 있다는 믿음이 실패 후 끈기를 높임"],"signalExpressions":["The most shocking thing","The honest answer is","So far, the best idea"]},{"section":"Call to Action: Be Gritty About Building Grit","startSegment":76,"endSegment":88,"summary":"Call to action: We need to be gritty about making kids grittier","keyPoints":["성장 마인드셋만으로는 부족, 더 많은 연구 필요","최고의 아이디어를 검증하고, 실패를 감수해야 함","재귀적 메시지: 그릿을 키우려면 우리 자신이 끈기를 가져야 함"],"signalExpressions":["But we need more","That''s where I''m going to end my remarks","In other words"]}],"signalExpressions":[{"expression":"When I was 27 years old","type":"narrative_opening","segmentIndex":0,"role":"개인 경험으로 시작하여 청중의 관심을 끄는 서사적 도입"},{"expression":"What struck me was that","type":"emphasis","segmentIndex":6,"role":"핵심 관찰을 강조하는 강조 구문"},{"expression":"And that got me thinking","type":"transition","segmentIndex":10,"role":"관찰에서 탐구로의 전환을 알리는 연결 표현"},{"expression":"But what if","type":"rhetorical_question","segmentIndex":21,"role":"기존 가정에 도전하는 수사적 질문으로 주제 전환"},{"expression":"In all those very different contexts","type":"summary","segmentIndex":36,"role":"다양한 연구를 종합하며 공통점을 도출하는 요약 표현"},{"expression":"It was grit","type":"reveal","segmentIndex":39,"role":"배제법 후 핵심 답을 공개하는 극적 전환점"},{"expression":"Turns out that","type":"evidence","segmentIndex":49,"role":"연구 결과를 구어체로 자연스럽게 보고하는 학술 표현"},{"expression":"The honest answer is","type":"honesty","segmentIndex":61,"role":"한계를 솔직히 인정하여 신뢰를 구축하는 고백 표현"},{"expression":"So far, the best idea I''ve heard","type":"recommendation","segmentIndex":67,"role":"겸손한 추천으로 성장 마인드셋 소개"},{"expression":"In other words","type":"restatement","segmentIndex":83,"role":"핵심 메시지를 한 문장으로 재정리하며 강연 마무리"}]}'::jsonb
)
