"listenDifficulty":2},{"index":48,"start":216.04,"end":219.26,"textEn":"Between related words in different Indo-European branches.","textKo":"서로 다른 인도유럽어 분파들의 관련 단어들 사이에서요.","words":[{"word":"between","start":216.04,"end":216.3},{"word":"related","start":216.3,"end":216.8},{"word":"words","start":216.8,"end":217.22},{"word":"in","start":217.22,"end":217.52},{"word":"different","start":217.52,"end":217.9},{"word":"Indo","start":217.9,"end":218.28},{"word":"-European","start":218.28,"end":218.88},{"word":"branches.","start":218.88,"end":219.26}],"listenDifficulty":2},{"index":49,"start":219.26,"end":225.06,"textEn":"Comparing English with Latin, we see that English has T where Latin has D,","textKo":"영어와 라틴어를 비교하면, 라틴어에 D가 있는 곳에 영어는 T가 있고,","words":[{"word":"Comparing","start":219.26,"end":220.6},{"word":"English","start":220.6,"end":221.0},{"word":"with","start":221.0,"end":221.22},{"word":"Latin,","start":221.22,"end":221.54},{"word":"we","start":221.88,"end":222.16},{"word":"see","start":222.16,"end":222.38},{"word":"that","start":222.38,"end":222.58},{"word":"English","start":222.58,"end":222.92},{"word":"has","start":222.92,"end":223.34},{"word":"T","start":223.34,"end":223.64},{"word":"where","start":223.64,"end":224.18},{"word":"Latin","start":224.18,"end":224.5},{"word":"has","start":224.5,"end":224.8},{"word":"D,","start":224.8,"end":225.06}],"listenDifficulty":1},{"index":50,"start":225.38,"end":228.96,"textEn":"And F where Latin has P at the start of words.","textKo":"단어 시작 부분에서 라틴어에 P가 있는 곳에 F가 있습니다.","words":[{"word":"and","start":225.38,"end":225.74},{"word":"F","start":225.74,"end":226.18},{"word":"where","start":226.18,"end":226.62},{"word":"Latin","start":226.62,"end":226.92},{"word":"has","start":226.92,"end":227.28},{"word":"P","start":227.28,"end":227.6},{"word":"at","start":227.6,"end":228.1},{"word":"the","start":228.1,"end":228.22},{"word":"start","start":228.22,"end":228.46},{"word":"of","start":228.46,"end":228.6},{"word":"words.","start":228.6,"end":228.96}],"listenDifficulty":2},{"index":51,"start":229.44,"end":233.56,"textEn":"Some of English''s more distant relatives include Hindi, Persian,","textKo":"영어의 더 먼 친척 언어로는 힌디어, 페르시아어,","words":[{"word":"Some","start":229.44,"end":229.88},{"word":"of","start":229.88,"end":230.0},{"word":"English''s","start":230.0,"end":230.48},{"word":"more","start":230.48,"end":230.72},{"word":"distant","start":230.72,"end":231.1},{"word":"relatives","start":231.1,"end":231.6},{"word":"include","start":231.6,"end":232.34},{"word":"Hindi,","start":232.34,"end":232.7},{"word":"Persian,","start":233.12,"end":233.56}],"listenDifficulty":1},{"index":52,"start":233.7,"end":237.24,"textEn":"And the Celtic languages it displaced in what is now Britain.","textKo":"그리고 현재 브리튼이 된 곳에서 대체한 켈트어가 있습니다.","words":[{"word":"and","start":233.7,"end":234.08},{"word":"the","start":234.08,"end":234.2},{"word":"Celtic","start":234.2,"end":234.62},{"word":"languages","start":234.62,"end":235.04},{"word":"it","start":235.04,"end":235.4},{"word":"displaced","start":235.4,"end":235.9},{"word":"in","start":235.9,"end":236.4},{"word":"what","start":236.4,"end":236.54},{"word":"is","start":236.54,"end":236.68},{"word":"now","start":236.68,"end":236.9},{"word":"Britain.","start":236.9,"end":237.24}],"listenDifficulty":3},{"index":53,"start":237.84,"end":242.38,"textEn":"Proto-Indo-European itself descended from an even more ancient language,","textKo":"인도유럽조어 자체도 훨씬 더 오래된 언어에서 파생되었지만,","words":[{"word":"Proto","start":237.84,"end":238.28},{"word":"-Indo","start":238.28,"end":238.66},{"word":"-European","start":238.66,"end":239.2},{"word":"itself","start":239.2,"end":239.72},{"word":"descended","start":239.72,"end":240.46},{"word":"from","start":240.46,"end":240.76},{"word":"an","start":240.76,"end":240.88},{"word":"even","start":240.88,"end":241.2},{"word":"more","start":241.2,"end":241.6},{"word":"ancient","start":241.6,"end":241.96},{"word":"language,","start":241.96,"end":242.38}],"listenDifficulty":1},{"index":54,"start":242.38,"end":247.76,"textEn":"But unfortunately, this is as far back as historical and archaeological evidence","textKo":"안타깝게도, 역사적, 고고학적 증거로","words":[{"word":"but","start":242.38,"end":242.94},{"word":"unfortunately,","start":242.94,"end":243.58},{"word":"this","start":243.9,"end":244.28},{"word":"is","start":244.28,"end":244.42},{"word":"as","start":244.42,"end":244.62},{"word":"far","start":244.62,"end":244.94},{"word":"back","start":244.94,"end":245.22},{"word":"as","start":245.22,"end":245.5},{"word":"historical","start":245.5,"end":246.08},{"word":"and","start":246.08,"end":246.44},{"word":"archaeological","start":246.44,"end":246.94},{"word":"evidence","start":246.94,"end":247.76}],"listenDifficulty":2},{"index":55,"start":247.76,"end":252.02,"textEn":"Will allow us to go. Many mysteries remain just out of reach,","textKo":"거슬러 올라갈 수 있는 한계입니다. 많은 미스터리가 여전히 풀리지 않은 채 남아 있으며,","words":[{"word":"will","start":247.76,"end":248.26},{"word":"allow","start":248.26,"end":248.46},{"word":"us","start":248.46,"end":248.66},{"word":"to","start":248.66,"end":248.78},{"word":"go.","start":248.78,"end":249.02},{"word":"Many","start":249.52,"end":250.16},{"word":"mysteries","start":250.16,"end":250.58},{"word":"remain","start":250.58,"end":251.04},{"word":"just","start":251.04,"end":251.36},{"word":"out","start":251.36,"end":251.56},{"word":"of","start":251.56,"end":251.68},{"word":"reach,","start":251.68,"end":252.02}],"listenDifficulty":3},{"index":56,"start":252.3,"end":255.58,"textEn":"Such as whether there might be a link between Indo-European","textKo":"예를 들어, 인도유럽어와","words":[{"word":"such","start":252.3,"end":252.62},{"word":"as","start":252.62,"end":252.76},{"word":"whether","start":252.76,"end":252.96},{"word":"there","start":252.96,"end":253.16},{"word":"might","start":253.16,"end":253.4},{"word":"be","start":253.4,"end":253.6},{"word":"a","start":253.6,"end":253.7},{"word":"link","start":253.7,"end":253.96},{"word":"between","start":253.96,"end":254.46},{"word":"Indo","start":254.46,"end":254.84},{"word":"-European","start":254.84,"end":255.58}],"listenDifficulty":3},{"index":57,"start":255.58,"end":262.0,"textEn":"And other major language families, and the nature of the languages spoken in Europe prior to its arrival.","textKo":"다른 주요 어족 사이에 연관이 있는지, 그리고 인도유럽어가 도착하기 전에 유럽에서 사용되던 언어의 본질 같은 것들입니다.","words":[{"word":"and","start":255.58,"end":256.02},{"word":"other","start":256.02,"end":256.32},{"word":"major","start":256.32,"end":256.62},{"word":"language","start":256.62,"end":256.94},{"word":"families,","start":256.94,"end":257.42},{"word":"and","start":257.62,"end":258.28},{"word":"the","start":258.28,"end":258.46},{"word":"nature","start":258.46,"end":258.76},{"word":"of","start":258.76,"end":258.94},{"word":"the","start":258.94,"end":259.04},{"word":"languages","start":259.04,"end":259.44},{"word":"spoken","start":259.44,"end":259.94},{"word":"in","start":259.94,"end":260.24},{"word":"Europe","start":260.24,"end":260.64},{"word":"prior","start":260.64,"end":261.24},{"word":"to","start":261.24,"end":261.48},{"word":"its","start":261.48,"end":261.64},{"word":"arrival.","start":261.64,"end":262.0}],"listenDifficulty":3},{"index":58,"start":262.18,"end":267.28,"textEn":"But the amazing fact remains that nearly three billion people around the world,","textKo":"하지만 놀라운 사실은 전 세계 거의 30억 명의 사람들이,","words":[{"word":"But","start":262.18,"end":262.66},{"word":"the","start":262.66,"end":262.8},{"word":"amazing","start":262.8,"end":263.16},{"word":"fact","start":263.16,"end":263.54},{"word":"remains","start":263.54,"end":264.1},{"word":"that","start":264.1,"end":264.62},{"word":"nearly","start":264.62,"end":264.94},{"word":"three","start":264.94,"end":265.24},{"word":"billion","start":265.24,"end":265.74},{"word":"people","start":265.74,"end":266.2},{"word":"around","start":266.2,"end":266.72},{"word":"the","start":266.72,"end":266.94},{"word":"world,","start":266.94,"end":267.28}],"listenDifficulty":2},{"index":59,"start":267.48,"end":269.72,"textEn":"Many of whom cannot understand each other,","textKo":"그 중 다수가 서로를 이해할 수 없지만,","words":[{"word":"many","start":267.48,"end":267.94},{"word":"of","start":267.94,"end":268.08},{"word":"whom","start":268.08,"end":268.28},{"word":"cannot","start":268.28,"end":268.6},{"word":"understand","start":268.6,"end":269.24},{"word":"each","start":269.24,"end":269.48},{"word":"other,","start":269.48,"end":269.72}],"listenDifficulty":3},{"index":60,"start":269.72,"end":275.76,"textEn":"Are nevertheless speaking the same words shaped by 6,000 years of history.","textKo":"그럼에도 불구하고 6,000년의 역사에 의해 형성된 같은 단어들을 말하고 있다는 것입니다.","words":[{"word":"are","start":269.72,"end":270.26},{"word":"nevertheless","start":270.26,"end":270.7},{"word":"speaking","start":270.7,"end":271.54},{"word":"the","start":271.54,"end":271.8},{"word":"same","start":271.8,"end":272.14},{"word":"words","start":272.14,"end":272.7},{"word":"shaped","start":272.7,"end":273.36},{"word":"by","start":273.36,"end":273.62},{"word":"6","start":273.62,"end":274.02},{"word":",000","start":274.02,"end":274.64},{"word":"years","start":274.64,"end":275.04},{"word":"of","start":275.04,"end":275.42},{"word":"history.","start":275.42,"end":275.76}],"listenDifficulty":3}]}'::jsonb, '[{"word":"dialect","phonetic":"/ˈdaɪəlekt/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"A regional or social variety of a language distinguished by pronunciation, grammar, or vocabulary.","meaningKo":"방언, 사투리","etymology":"From Greek ''dialektos'' meaning ''discourse, way of speaking''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"dia-","root":"lect","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["dialectal","dialectic"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":1,"contextSentence":"But what do the dialects spoken in dozens of countries around the world have in common?"},{"word":"evolved","phonetic":"/ɪˈvɒlvd/","partOfSpeech":"verb","definition":"Developed gradually over time, especially from a simple to a more complex form.","meaningKo":"진화하다, 발전하다","etymology":"From Latin ''evolvere'' meaning ''to unroll, unfold''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"e-","root":"volv","suffix":"-ed"},"relatedWords":["evolution","evolving","revolve"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":5,"contextSentence":"English has evolved through generations of speakers."},{"word":"trace","phonetic":"/treɪs/","partOfSpeech":"verb","definition":"To find the origin or course of development of something.","meaningKo":"추적하다, 더듬어 가다","etymology":"From Old French ''tracier'' meaning ''to follow, track''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"trace","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["traceable","retracing","tracing"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":7,"contextSentence":"We can trace the language from the present day back to its ancient roots."},{"word":"invasion","phonetic":"/ɪnˈveɪʒən/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"An instance of invading a country or region with an armed force.","meaningKo":"침략, 침공","etymology":"From Latin ''invasionem'' meaning ''an attack, invasion''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"in-","root":"vas","suffix":"-ion"},"relatedWords":["invade","invader","invasive"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":11,"contextSentence":"They started coming into the language with the Norman invasion of England in 1066."},{"word":"conquered","phonetic":"/ˈkɒŋkərd/","partOfSpeech":"verb","definition":"Overcame and took control of a place or people by military force.","meaningKo":"정복하다","etymology":"From Old French ''conquerre'', from Latin ''conquirere'' meaning ''to seek out, procure''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"con-","root":"quer","suffix":"-ed"},"relatedWords":["conquer","conquest","conqueror"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":12,"contextSentence":"When the French-speaking Normans conquered England."},{"word":"massive","phonetic":"/ˈmæsɪv/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"Exceptionally large or extensive.","meaningKo":"막대한, 거대한","etymology":"From French ''massif'', from Latin ''massa'' meaning ''lump, mass''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"mass","suffix":"-ive"},"relatedWords":["mass","massively","massiveness"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":14,"contextSentence":"Adding a massive amount of French and Latin vocabulary."},{"word":"vocabulary","phonetic":"/vəˈkæbjʊləri/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"The body of words used in a particular language or known to an individual.","meaningKo":"어휘, 단어","etymology":"From Medieval Latin ''vocabularium'', from Latin ''vocabulum'' meaning ''word, name''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"vocab","suffix":"-ulary"},"relatedWords":["vocal","vocalize","advocate"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":14,"contextSentence":"Adding a massive amount of French and Latin vocabulary."},{"word":"comparative","phonetic":"/kəmˈpærətɪv/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"Involving comparison between two or more things.","meaningKo":"비교의, 비교적인","etymology":"From Latin ''comparativus'', from ''comparare'' meaning ''to compare''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"com-","root":"par","suffix":"-ative"},"relatedWords":["compare","comparison","comparable"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":26,"contextSentence":"But comparative linguistics can help us by focusing on grammatical structure."},{"word":"linguistics","phonetic":"/lɪŋˈɡwɪstɪks/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"The scientific study of language and its structure.","meaningKo":"언어학","etymology":"From Latin ''lingua'' meaning ''language, tongue''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"lingu","suffix":"-istics"},"relatedWords":["linguist","linguistic","bilingual"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":26,"contextSentence":"But comparative linguistics can help us by focusing on grammatical structure."},{"word":"grammatical","phonetic":"/ɡrəˈmætɪkəl/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"Relating to grammar or conforming to the rules of grammar.","meaningKo":"문법의, 문법적인","etymology":"From Greek ''grammatikos'' meaning ''of letters''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"gramm","suffix":"-atical"},"relatedWords":["grammar","grammarian","grammatically"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":26,"contextSentence":"Comparative linguistics can help us by focusing on grammatical structure."},{"word":"systematically","phonetic":"/ˌsɪstəˈmætɪkli/","partOfSpeech":"adverb","definition":"According to a fixed plan or system; in a methodical way.","meaningKo":"체계적으로","etymology":"From Greek ''systematikos'' meaning ''combined in a system''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"system","suffix":"-atically"},"relatedWords":["system","systematic","systematize"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":29,"contextSentence":"German words starting with P systematically shifted to a PF sound."},{"word":"counterparts","phonetic":"/ˈkaʊntəpɑːrts/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"A person or thing that corresponds to or has the same function as another in a different context.","meaningKo":"대응물, 상대","etymology":"From ''counter'' (opposite) + ''part''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"counter-","root":"part","suffix":"-s"},"relatedWords":["counterpart","counterpoint","counterbalance"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":30,"contextSentence":"While their Old English counterparts kept the P unchanged."},{"word":"borrowings","phonetic":"/ˈbɒroʊɪŋz/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"Words adopted from one language into another.","meaningKo":"차용어","etymology":"From Old English ''borgian'' meaning ''to lend, borrow''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"borrow","suffix":"-ings"},"relatedWords":["borrow","borrowed","borrower"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":23,"contextSentence":"Viking invaders added more borrowings from Old Norse into the mix."},{"word":"descended","phonetic":"/dɪˈsendɪd/","partOfSpeech":"verb","definition":"Originated or derived from an ancestor or earlier form.","meaningKo":"파생되다, 유래하다","etymology":"From Latin ''descendere'' meaning ''to come down, go down''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"de-","root":"scend","suffix":"-ed"},"relatedWords":["descend","descendant","descent"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":34,"contextSentence":"Just as the various Romance languages descended from Latin."},{"word":"ancestor","phonetic":"/ˈænsestər/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"A person or thing from which later forms developed; a forefather.","meaningKo":"조상, 선조","etymology":"From Old French ''ancestre'', from Latin ''antecessor'' meaning ''predecessor''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"ante-","root":"cess","suffix":"-or"},"relatedWords":["ancestry","ancestral"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":36,"contextSentence":"Descended from their own common ancestor, known as Proto-Germanic."},{"word":"reconstruct","phonetic":"/ˌriːkənˈstrʌkt/","partOfSpeech":"verb","definition":"To build or form again; to re-create or re-establish based on available evidence.","meaningKo":"재구성하다, 복원하다","etymology":"From Latin ''re-'' (again) + ''construere'' (to build)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"re-","root":"con-struct","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["reconstruction","construct","structure"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":38,"contextSentence":"We can only reconstruct it by comparing its descendants."},{"word":"consistency","phonetic":"/kənˈsɪstənsi/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"The quality of being unchanging or achieving the same standard repeatedly.","meaningKo":"일관성","etymology":"From Latin ''consistentia'' meaning ''standing firm''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"con-","root":"sist","suffix":"-ency"},"relatedWords":["consistent","consist","inconsistency"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":39,"contextSentence":"Which is possible thanks to the consistency of the changes."},{"word":"correspondences","phonetic":"/ˌkɒrɪˈspɒndənsɪz/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"Close similarities or connections between things; systematic relationships between sounds in related languages.","meaningKo":"대응 관계, 유사점","etymology":"From Medieval Latin ''correspondentia'', from ''correspondere'' meaning ''to respond together''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"cor-","root":"respond","suffix":"-ences"},"relatedWords":["correspond","corresponding","correspondent"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":47,"contextSentence":"We can find the same systematic similarities, or correspondences."},{"word":"displaced","phonetic":"/dɪsˈpleɪst/","partOfSpeech":"verb","definition":"Took the place of; forced something out of its position or role.","meaningKo":"대체하다, 밀어내다","etymology":"From Old French ''desplacer'', meaning ''to move from a place''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"dis-","root":"place","suffix":"-d"},"relatedWords":["displace","displacement","replace"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":52,"contextSentence":"And the Celtic languages it displaced in what is now Britain."},{"word":"archaeological","phonetic":"/ˌɑːrkiəˈlɒdʒɪkəl/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"Relating to the study of human history through excavation and analysis of physical remains.","meaningKo":"고고학적인","etymology":"From Greek ''arkhaios'' (ancient) + ''logos'' (study)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"archaeo-","root":"log","suffix":"-ical"},"relatedWords":["archaeology","archaeologist","archaic"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":54,"contextSentence":"This is as far back as historical and archaeological evidence will allow us to go."},{"word":"nevertheless","phonetic":"/ˌnevəðəˈles/","partOfSpeech":"adverb","definition":"In spite of that; however; all the same.","meaningKo":"그럼에도 불구하고","etymology":"From ''never'' + ''the'' + ''less'', Middle English compound","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"never","root":"the","suffix":"less"},"relatedWords":["nonetheless","however","notwithstanding"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":60,"contextSentence":"Are nevertheless speaking the same words shaped by 6,000 years of history."}]'::jsonb, '[{"pattern":"Present Perfect (has + past participle)","explanation":"Used to describe an action that started in the past and continues to the present, or an action with present relevance. Here ''has evolved'' shows an ongoing historical process.","explanationKo":"과거에 시작되어 현재까지 계속되는 동작이나 현재와 관련이 있는 과거 동작을 나타냅니다. 여기서 ''has evolved''는 지속적인 역사적 과정을 보여줍니다.","examples":["English has evolved through generations of speakers.","The language has changed over many centuries."],"segmentIndex":5},{"pattern":"Passive Voice (was/were + past participle)","explanation":"Used when the focus is on the action or result rather than who performed it. Common in academic and historical writing to describe events objectively.","explanationKo":"행위자보다 동작이나 결과에 초점을 맞출 때 사용합니다. 학술적, 역사적 서술에서 사건을 객관적으로 묘사할 때 흔히 쓰입니다.","examples":["This historical language was never written down.","First brought to the British Isles in the 5th and 6th centuries."],"segmentIndex":37},{"pattern":"By + gerund (means/method)","explanation":"The structure ''by + verb-ing'' is used to describe the method or means by which something is achieved.","explanationKo":"''by + 동명사'' 구조는 어떤 것을 달성하는 방법이나 수단을 설명할 때 사용합니다.","examples":["By undoing these changes, we can trace the language.","By focusing on grammatical structure.","By comparing its descendants."],"segmentIndex":7},{"pattern":"While + contrast clause","explanation":"Used to introduce a contrasting idea. ''While'' connects two clauses that present different or opposing information.","explanationKo":"''While''은 대조적인 내용을 소개할 때 사용합니다. 서로 다르거나 반대되는 정보를 제시하는 두 절을 연결합니다.","examples":["While modern English shares many similar words with Latin-derived Romance languages, most of those words were not originally part of it.","While their Old English counterparts kept the P unchanged."],"segmentIndex":8},{"pattern":"Modal verb ''may/might'' for possibility","explanation":"''May'' and ''might'' express possibility or uncertainty. ''May'' suggests a slightly higher probability than ''might''.","explanationKo":"''may''와 ''might''는 가능성이나 불확실성을 표현합니다. ''may''가 ''might''보다 약간 더 높은 확률을 나타냅니다.","examples":["It may be hard to see the roots of modern English.","It might be more recognizable if you know some German.","Whether there might be a link between Indo-European and other major language families."],"segmentIndex":24},{"pattern":"Conditional with ''if'' clause","explanation":"An ''if'' clause introduces a condition. Here it presents a hypothetical situation that would change the outcome.","explanationKo":"''if'' 절은 조건을 도입합니다. 여기서는 결과를 바꿀 수 있는 가정적 상황을 제시합니다.","examples":["It might be more recognizable if you know some German."],"segmentIndex":18},{"pattern":"Relative clause with ''that/which/who''","explanation":"Relative clauses add extra information about a noun. ''That'' defines or restricts, ''which'' adds non-essential information, and ''who'' refers to people.","explanationKo":"관계절은 명사에 대한 추가 정보를 더합니다. ''that''은 제한적으로 정의하고, ''which''는 비필수적 정보를 추가하며, ''who''는 사람을 가리킵니다.","examples":["Words that have SK sounds in Swedish developed an SH sound in English.","Which is possible thanks to the consistency of the changes.","Many of whom cannot understand each other."],"segmentIndex":31},{"pattern":"Would + base verb (past future/habitual past)","explanation":"Used to talk about future events from a past perspective, or to describe habitual actions in the past.","explanationKo":"과거 시점에서 미래의 사건을 이야기하거나, 과거의 습관적인 행동을 묘사할 때 사용합니다.","examples":["The Germanic dialects they spoke would become known as Anglo-Saxon."],"segmentIndex":22},{"pattern":"Just as... so too (comparison)","explanation":"Used to draw a parallel between two similar situations. ''Just as'' introduces the first situation, and the second follows with an implied or explicit ''so too''.","explanationKo":"두 가지 유사한 상황 사이에 유사점을 도출할 때 사용합니다. ''just as''가 첫 번째 상황을 소개하고, 두 번째 상황이 암시적 또는 명시적 ''so too''와 함께 따릅니다.","examples":["Just as the various Romance languages descended from Latin, English, Swedish, German descended from their own common ancestor."],"segmentIndex":34},{"pattern":"Known as + noun (naming/identification)","explanation":"Used to introduce an alternative name or designation for something previously mentioned.","explanationKo":"이전에 언급된 것의 대안적 이름이나 명칭을 소개할 때 사용합니다.","examples":["Known as Proto-Germanic, spoken around 500 BCE.","Would become known as Anglo-Saxon."],"segmentIndex":36},{"pattern":"Such as + examples","explanation":"''Such as'' introduces specific examples to illustrate a general statement.","explanationKo":"''such as''는 일반적인 진술을 설명하기 위해 구체적인 예를 도입합니다.","examples":["Such as whether there might be a link between Indo-European and other major language families."],"segmentIndex":56},{"pattern":"As well as (addition)","explanation":"''As well as'' is used to add additional information, meaning ''in addition to'' or ''and also''.","explanationKo":"''as well as''는 ''~뿐만 아니라'' 또는 ''~도 또한''이라는 의미로 추가 정보를 더할 때 사용합니다.","examples":["As well as large parts of southern and western Asia."],"segmentIndex":45}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"linking","originalText":"think of it","phonetic":"/θɪŋk_əv_ɪt/","koreanPhonetic":"씽커빗","explanation":"The ''k'' in ''think'' links to the ''o'' in ''of'', and ''of'' reduces to /əv/ linking smoothly to ''it''.","explanationKo":"''think''의 ''k''가 ''of''의 모음과 연결되고, ''of''가 /əv/로 약화되어 ''it''과 매끄럽게 이어집니다.","practiceGuide":"''씽커빗''으로 빠르게 연결하여 발음하세요. ''of''를 약하게 ''어''로 발음합니다.","segmentIndex":0},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"dozens of","phonetic":"/ˈdʌznzəv/","koreanPhonetic":"더즌저브","explanation":"''Of'' is reduced to /əv/ and joins closely with the preceding word, sounding almost like one word.","explanationKo":"''of''가 /əv/로 축약되어 앞 단어와 밀접하게 붙어 거의 한 단어처럼 들립니다.","practiceGuide":"''더즌즈''와 ''어브''를 빠르게 붙여 ''더즌저브''로 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":1},{"type":"linking","originalText":"most of","phonetic":"/moʊstəv/","koreanPhonetic":"모우스터브","explanation":"The ''t'' in ''most'' links directly into the reduced ''of'' /əv/, creating a smooth connection.","explanationKo":"''most''의 ''t''가 축약된 ''of'' /əv/에 바로 연결되어 매끄러운 이음을 만듭니다.","practiceGuide":"''모우스트''와 ''어브''를 자연스럽게 이어서 ''모우스터브''로 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":9},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"a massive amount of","phonetic":"/ə ˈmæsɪv əˈmaʊntəv/","koreanPhonetic":"어 매시브 어마운터브","explanation":"The article ''a'' reduces to schwa /ə/, and ''of'' reduces to /əv/, blending with ''amount''.","explanationKo":"관사 ''a''가 슈와 /ə/로 축약되고, ''of''가 /əv/로 축약되어 ''amount''와 섞입니다.","practiceGuide":"''a''를 아주 약하게 ''어''로 발음하고, ''amount of''를 빠르게 ''어마운터브''로 이어 말하세요.","segmentIndex":14},{"type":"assimilation","originalText":"can help","phonetic":"/kən_help/ → /kəŋ_help/","koreanPhonetic":"컹 헬프","explanation":"The ''n'' in ''can'' often assimilates toward the following ''h'', and ''can'' is reduced to /kən/ in unstressed position.","explanationKo":"''can''의 ''n''이 뒤따르는 ''h'' 앞에서 동화되며, 강세가 없는 위치에서 ''can''이 /kən/으로 축약됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''can''을 강하게 ''캔''으로 발음하지 말고, 약하게 ''컨''으로 발음하여 ''help''에 이어 말하세요.","segmentIndex":26},{"type":"elision","originalText":"doesn''t look","phonetic":"/ˈdʌzn̩t lʊk/","koreanPhonetic":"더즌(트) 룩","explanation":"The ''t'' at the end of ''doesn''t'' is often dropped or unreleased before the ''l'' in ''look''.","explanationKo":"''doesn''t''의 끝 ''t''가 ''look''의 ''l'' 앞에서 탈락하거나 파열되지 않습니다.","practiceGuide":"''doesn''t''의 마지막 ''t''를 거의 발음하지 말고, 바로 ''look''으로 넘어가세요.","segmentIndex":17},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"would become","phonetic":"/wʊd bɪˈkʌm/ → /wəd bɪˈkʌm/","koreanPhonetic":"워드 비컴","explanation":"''Would'' is reduced in fast speech from /wʊd/ to /wəd/ or even /wəd/, losing the full vowel sound.","explanationKo":"빠른 말에서 ''would''가 /wʊd/에서 /wəd/로 축약되어 완전한 모음 소리를 잃습니다.","practiceGuide":"''would''를 ''우드''로 강하게 발음하지 말고, 약하게 ''워드''로 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":22},{"type":"linking","originalText":"added more","phonetic":"/ˈædɪd_mɔːr/","koreanPhonetic":"애디드 모어","explanation":"The final ''d'' of ''added'' links to ''m'' of ''more'' with a brief stop-to-nasal transition.","explanationKo":"''added''의 마지막 ''d''가 ''more''의 ''m''과 연결되면서 짧은 파열-비음 전환이 일어납니다.","practiceGuide":"''added''의 ''d''를 가볍게 발음하면서 바로 ''more''로 연결하세요.","segmentIndex":23},{"type":"linking","originalText":"out of reach","phonetic":"/aʊt_əv_riːtʃ/","koreanPhonetic":"아우터브 리치","explanation":"The ''t'' in ''out'' links to reduced ''of'' /əv/, and then to ''reach''. The three words flow together.","explanationKo":"''out''의 ''t''가 축약된 ''of'' /əv/에 연결되고, 이어서 ''reach''로 이어집니다. 세 단어가 함께 흐릅니다.","practiceGuide":"''아웃 오브 리치''가 아니라 ''아우터브리치''로 연결하여 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":55},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"them related to the","phonetic":"/ðəm rɪˈleɪtɪd tə ðə/","koreanPhonetic":"덤 릴레이리드 터 더","explanation":"''Them'' reduces to /ðəm/, ''to'' reduces to /tə/, and ''the'' reduces to /ðə/ in fast speech.","explanationKo":"빠른 말에서 ''them''이 /ðəm/으로, ''to''가 /tə/로, ''the''가 /ðə/로 축약됩니다.","practiceGuide":"기능어들을 약하게 발음하고 내용어인 ''related''에 강세를 두세요.","segmentIndex":3},{"type":"assimilation","originalText":"written down","phonetic":"/ˈrɪtn̩ daʊn/","koreanPhonetic":"뤼튼 다운","explanation":"The double ''t'' in ''written'' is reduced to a glottal stop, and the syllabic ''n'' connects to ''down''.","explanationKo":"''written''의 ''tt''가 성문 파열음으로 축약되고, 음절적 ''n''이 ''down''과 연결됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''written''의 ''t''를 가볍게 끊듯 발음하고 바로 ''down''으로 이어가세요.","segmentIndex":37},{"type":"linking","originalText":"same words shaped","phonetic":"/seɪm wɜːrdz ʃeɪpt/","koreanPhonetic":"세임 워즈 셰잎트","explanation":"The ''m'' in ''same'' connects to ''w'' of ''words'', and the ''z'' of ''words'' links to ''sh'' of ''shaped''.","explanationKo":"''same''의 ''m''이 ''words''의 ''w''와 연결되고, ''words''의 ''z''가 ''shaped''의 ''sh''와 이어집니다.","practiceGuide":"세 단어를 끊지 않고 자연스럽게 연결하여 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":60},{"type":"elision","originalText":"just as","phonetic":"/dʒʌs_æz/","koreanPhonetic":"저스 애즈","explanation":"The ''t'' in ''just'' is often dropped before the vowel of ''as'' in connected speech.","explanationKo":"연속된 말에서 ''just''의 ''t''가 ''as''의 모음 앞에서 종종 탈락합니다.","practiceGuide":"''just''의 ''t''를 거의 발음하지 않고 바로 ''as''로 이어가세요. ''저스애즈''로 발음합니다.","segmentIndex":34},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"can only","phonetic":"/kən ˈoʊnli/","koreanPhonetic":"컨 오운리","explanation":"''Can'' is reduced to /kən/ (unstressed), with the schwa vowel, before the stressed word ''only''.","explanationKo":"''can''이 강세 없는 위치에서 슈와 모음을 가진 /kən/으로 축약되어 강세가 있는 ''only'' 앞에 옵니다.","practiceGuide":"''캔''이 아니라 ''컨''으로 약하게 발음하고, ''only''에 강세를 두세요.","segmentIndex":38},{"type":"linking","originalText":"prior to its","phonetic":"/praɪər tə_ɪts/","koreanPhonetic":"프라이어 터 잇츠","explanation":"''To'' reduces to /tə/ and links smoothly into ''its''. The vowel sounds flow together.","explanationKo":"''to''가 /tə/로 축약되어 ''its''와 매끄럽게 연결됩니다. 모음 소리가 함께 흐릅니다.","practiceGuide":"''to''를 ''투''가 아닌 ''터''로 발음하고, ''its''와 자연스럽게 이어 말하세요.","segmentIndex":57}]'::jsonb, '{"sections":[{"section":"Introduction - The Question","startSegment":0,"endSegment":3,"summary":"Poses the central question: what do diverse English dialects, Chaucer''s writings, and Beowulf have in common?","summaryKo":"핵심 질문을 제시합니다: 다양한 영어 방언, 초서의 글, 베오울프가 어떤 공통점을 가지고 있을까요?","keyPoints":["영어를 단일 언어로 생각하지만 실제로는 다양한 방언이 있음","현대 영어와 고대 문학 사이의 관계에 대한 질문 제기"]},{"section":"Core Answer - Language Evolution","startSegment":4,"endSegment":9,"summary":"Explains that English, like most languages, has evolved over generations. Modern English shares words with Romance languages, but most were borrowed later.","summaryKo":"영어가 대부분의 언어처럼 세대를 거치며 진화해 왔음을 설명합니다. 현대 영어가 로망스어와 단어를 공유하지만, 대부분은 나중에 차용된 것입니다.","keyPoints":["영어는 여러 세대에 걸쳐 진화해 왔음","라틴어 파생 로망스어와 유사한 단어는 원래 영어의 일부가 아니었음"]},{"section":"Norman Invasion and French Influence","startSegment":10,"endSegment":16,"summary":"Describes how the Norman invasion of 1066 brought French and Latin vocabulary into Old English.","summaryKo":"1066년 노르만 침공이 프랑스어와 라틴어 어휘를 고대 영어에 가져온 과정을 설명합니다.","keyPoints":["1066년 노르만족의 잉글랜드 침공","프랑스어를 사용하는 지배 계층이 대량의 프랑스어와 라틴어 어휘를 추가","침공 이전의 언어를 고대 영어라고 부름"]},{"section":"Old English and Germanic Roots","startSegment":17,"endSegment":23,"summary":"Explains Old English''s Germanic origins, brought by Angles, Saxons, and Jutes, with additional Norse borrowings from Viking invasions.","summaryKo":"앵글족, 색슨족, 주트족이 가져온 고대 영어의 게르만어 기원과 바이킹 침략으로 인한 노르드어 차용을 설명합니다.","keyPoints":["고대 영어는 게르만어족에 속함","5~6세기에 앵글족, 색슨족, 주트족이 브리튼 제도에 가져옴","8~11세기 바이킹 침략자들이 고대 노르드어 차용어를 추가"]},{"section":"Comparative Linguistics Method","startSegment":24,"endSegment":33,"summary":"Introduces comparative linguistics as a tool to trace language origins through grammatical patterns, sound changes, and core vocabulary, with specific examples of P-to-PF and SK-to-SH shifts.","summaryKo":"문법 패턴, 음운 변화, 핵심 어휘를 통해 언어 기원을 추적하는 도구로서 비교언어학을 소개하며, P에서 PF로, SK에서 SH로의 변화 등 구체적 예를 제시합니다.","keyPoints":["비교언어학은 문법 구조, 음운 변화 패턴, 핵심 어휘에 초점","독일어의 P에서 PF로의 변화 vs 영어의 P 유지","스웨덴어 SK가 영어에서 SH로 변화","skirt, skull 같은 단어는 SK-SH 변화 이후의 노르드어 차용어"]},{"section":"Proto-Germanic and Proto-Indo-European","startSegment":34,"endSegment":45,"summary":"Traces the language family tree from Proto-Germanic (500 BCE) to Proto-Indo-European (6,000 years ago), encompassing nearly all European and many Asian languages.","summaryKo":"원시 게르만어(기원전 500년)에서 인도유럽조어(6,000년 전)까지의 언어 가계도를 추적하며, 거의 모든 유럽 언어와 많은 아시아 언어를 포괄합니다.","keyPoints":["영어, 스웨덴어, 독일어 등은 원시 게르만어(기원전 500년경)에서 파생","원시 게르만어는 기록된 적이 없어 후손 언어 비교로만 재구성 가능","원시 게르만어는 인도유럽조어에서 유래","인도유럽조어는 약 6,000년 전 폰투스 초원에서 사용됨"]},{"section":"Sound Correspondences Across Indo-European","startSegment":46,"endSegment":52,"summary":"Demonstrates systematic sound correspondences between English and Latin (T/D, F/P), and mentions distant relatives like Hindi, Persian, and Celtic languages.","summaryKo":"영어와 라틴어 사이의 체계적 음운 대응(T/D, F/P)을 보여주고, 힌디어, 페르시아어, 켈트어 등 먼 친척 언어를 언급합니다.","keyPoints":["영어 T = 라틴어 D, 영어 F = 라틴어 P (단어 시작 부분)","영어의 먼 친척: 힌디어, 페르시아어, 켈트어"]},{"section":"Conclusion - Remaining Mysteries and Wonder","startSegment":53,"endSegment":60,"summary":"Acknowledges the limits of historical evidence while marveling at the fact that nearly three billion people speak words shaped by 6,000 years of shared history.","summaryKo":"역사적 증거의 한계를 인정하면서도, 거의 30억 명이 6,000년의 공유된 역사에 의해 형성된 단어들을 사용하고 있다는 놀라운 사실을 강조합니다.","keyPoints":["인도유럽조어 이전의 언어는 추적 불가능","인도유럽어와 다른 어족 사이의 연결 등 미스터리가 남아 있음","거의 30억 명이 서로 이해할 수 없지만 6,000년 역사로 형성된 같은 단어들을 사용"]}],"signalExpressions":[{"expression":"When we talk about English, we often think of it as a single language.","type":"hook","segmentIndex":0,"role":"일반적 인식을 제시하여 청중의 흥미를 유발하는 도입부"},{"expression":"But what do the dialects spoken in dozens of countries...","type":"hook","segmentIndex":1,"role":"수사적 질문으로 호기심을 자극하며 영상의 핵심 주제를 제시"},{"expression":"The answer is that","type":"transition","segmentIndex":4,"role":"질문에서 설명으로 전환하는 신호 표현"},{"expression":"Instead","type":"transition","segmentIndex":10,"role":"예상과 다른 사실을 소개하는 전환 표현"},{"expression":"Today, we call that language Old English.","type":"transition","segmentIndex":16,"role":"역사적 배경에서 구체적인 언어학 용어로 전환"},{"expression":"That''s because","type":"transition","segmentIndex":19,"role":"이유를 설명하는 인과관계 표현"},{"expression":"But comparative linguistics can help us","type":"transition","segmentIndex":26,"role":"문제에서 해결책(비교언어학)으로 전환하는 표현"},{"expression":"For example","type":"transition","segmentIndex":28,"role":"구체적인 예시를 도입하는 신호 표현"},{"expression":"These examples show us that","type":"transition","segmentIndex":34,"role":"구체적 예시에서 일반적 결론으로 전환"},{"expression":"We can even use the same process to go back one step further","type":"transition","segmentIndex":40,"role":"논의를 더 깊은 단계로 확장하는 전환 표현"},{"expression":"But unfortunately, this is as far back as... will allow us to go.","type":"transition","segmentIndex":54,"role":"연구의 한계를 인정하며 결론부로 전환"},{"expression":"But the amazing fact remains that","type":"conclusion","segmentIndex":58,"role":"한계에도 불구하고 놀라운 결론을 제시하는 마무리 표현"},{"expression":"Are nevertheless speaking the same words shaped by 6,000 years of history.","type":"conclusion","segmentIndex":60,"role":"6,000년의 공유 역사를 강조하며 감동적으로 마무리"}]}'::jsonb
)
