small step.","textKo":"그리고 그 모든 것은 단 하나의 작은 발걸음에서 시작되었어요.","words":[{"word":"And","start":870.18,"end":870.62},{"word":"it","start":870.62,"end":870.72},{"word":"all","start":870.72,"end":870.86},{"word":"started","start":870.86,"end":871.26},{"word":"with","start":871.26,"end":871.9},{"word":"just","start":871.9,"end":872.06},{"word":"one","start":872.06,"end":872.32},{"word":"small","start":872.32,"end":872.72},{"word":"step.","start":872.72,"end":873.14}],"listenDifficulty":1},{"index":176,"start":875.28,"end":879.96,"textEn":"So I invite you to consider what bad mental habits are holding you back.","textKo":"그래서 여러분에게 어떤 나쁜 정신적 습관이 여러분을 막고 있는지 생각해 보시라고 권해요.","words":[{"word":"So","start":875.28,"end":875.96},{"word":"I","start":875.96,"end":876.08},{"word":"invite","start":876.08,"end":876.28},{"word":"you","start":876.28,"end":876.42},{"word":"to","start":876.42,"end":876.54},{"word":"consider","start":876.54,"end":877.02},{"word":"what","start":877.02,"end":877.98},{"word":"bad","start":877.98,"end":878.26},{"word":"mental","start":878.26,"end":878.6},{"word":"habits","start":878.6,"end":879.0},{"word":"are","start":879.0,"end":879.2},{"word":"holding","start":879.2,"end":879.48},{"word":"you","start":879.48,"end":879.7},{"word":"back.","start":879.7,"end":879.96}],"listenDifficulty":1},{"index":177,"start":881.12,"end":886.52,"textEn":"What unhealthy beliefs are keeping you from being as mentally strong as you could be?","textKo":"어떤 건강하지 못한 믿음이 여러분이 최대한 정신적으로 강해지는 것을 막고 있나요?","words":[{"word":"What","start":881.12,"end":881.8},{"word":"unhealthy","start":881.8,"end":882.24},{"word":"beliefs","start":882.24,"end":882.82},{"word":"are","start":882.82,"end":883.54},{"word":"keeping","start":883.54,"end":883.82},{"word":"you","start":883.82,"end":884.02},{"word":"from","start":884.02,"end":884.2},{"word":"being","start":884.2,"end":884.5},{"word":"as","start":884.5,"end":885.02},{"word":"mentally","start":885.02,"end":885.32},{"word":"strong","start":885.32,"end":885.66},{"word":"as","start":885.66,"end":885.9},{"word":"you","start":885.9,"end":886.02},{"word":"could","start":886.02,"end":886.26},{"word":"be?","start":886.26,"end":886.52}],"listenDifficulty":1},{"index":178,"start":887.5,"end":893.48,"textEn":"And what''s one small step that you could take today, right here, right now?","textKo":"그리고 오늘, 바로 여기, 바로 지금 취할 수 있는 작은 발걸음 하나가 무엇인가요?","words":[{"word":"And","start":887.5,"end":888.18},{"word":"what''s","start":888.18,"end":888.44},{"word":"one","start":888.44,"end":888.8},{"word":"small","start":888.8,"end":889.32},{"word":"step","start":889.32,"end":889.68},{"word":"that","start":889.68,"end":889.88},{"word":"you","start":889.88,"end":890.02},{"word":"could","start":890.02,"end":890.22},{"word":"take","start":890.22,"end":890.5},{"word":"today,","start":890.5,"end":890.88},{"word":"right","start":891.28,"end":891.82},{"word":"here,","start":891.82,"end":892.24},{"word":"right","start":892.58,"end":893.12},{"word":"now?","start":893.12,"end":893.48}],"listenDifficulty":1},{"index":179,"start":894.06,"end":894.98,"textEn":"Thank you.","textKo":"감사합니다.","words":[{"word":"Thank","start":894.06,"end":894.74},{"word":"you.","start":894.74,"end":894.98}],"listenDifficulty":1}]}'::jsonb, '[{"word":"rewarding","phonetic":"/rɪˈwɔːrdɪŋ/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"providing satisfaction; gratifying","meaningKo":"보람 있는, 만족스러운","etymology":"From Old French ''regarder'' (to regard), via Anglo-Norman French ''rewarder''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"re-","root":"ward","suffix":"-ing"},"relatedWords":["reward","unrewarding","rewardingly"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":1,"contextSentence":"She lives in a gorgeous house, and she has a really rewarding career."},{"word":"gorgeous","phonetic":"/ˈɡɔːrdʒəs/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"beautiful; very attractive","meaningKo":"아주 멋진, 매우 아름다운","etymology":"From Old French ''gorgias'' (elegant, fashionable)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"gorge","suffix":"-ous"},"relatedWords":["gorgeously","gorgeousness"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":1,"contextSentence":"She lives in a gorgeous house, and she has a really rewarding career."},{"word":"blessed","phonetic":"/blɛst/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"favored by God or fortune; fortunate","meaningKo":"축복받은, 행운의","etymology":"From Old English ''blētsian'' (to consecrate with blood)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"bless","suffix":"-ed"},"relatedWords":["blessing","bless","unblessed"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":5,"contextSentence":"And she''s always posting about how blessed she is."},{"word":"destructive","phonetic":"/dɪˈstrʌktɪv/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"causing great damage or harm","meaningKo":"파괴적인, 해로운","etymology":"From Latin ''destructivus'', from ''destruere'' (to tear down)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"de-","root":"struct","suffix":"-ive"},"relatedWords":["destruction","destroy","constructive"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":24,"contextSentence":"There''s three kinds of destructive beliefs."},{"word":"magnify","phonetic":"/ˈmæɡnɪfaɪ/","partOfSpeech":"verb","definition":"to make something appear larger or more important than it is","meaningKo":"확대하다, 과장하다","etymology":"From Latin ''magnificare'' (to esteem greatly), from ''magnus'' (great)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"magn-","suffix":"-ify"},"relatedWords":["magnification","magnificent","magnitude"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":29,"contextSentence":"It''s when you start to magnify your misfortune."},{"word":"misfortune","phonetic":"/mɪsˈfɔːrtʃuːn/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"bad luck; an unfortunate event or circumstance","meaningKo":"불행, 불운","etymology":"From Old French ''mesfortune'', from ''mes-'' (bad) + ''fortune''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"mis-","root":"fortune","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["fortune","unfortunate","misfortunate"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":29,"contextSentence":"It''s when you start to magnify your misfortune."},{"word":"self-pity","phonetic":"/ˌsɛlfˈpɪti/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"excessive concern with and unhappiness over one''s own troubles","meaningKo":"자기연민, 자기 불쌍히 여김","etymology":"Compound of ''self'' (Old English) + ''pity'' (Old French ''pité'')","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"self-","root":"pity","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["self-pitying","pitiful","pitiable"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":29,"contextSentence":"Self-pity goes beyond that."},{"word":"consequences","phonetic":"/ˈkɑːnsɪkwɛnsɪz/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"results or effects of an action or condition","meaningKo":"결과, 영향","etymology":"From Latin ''consequentia'', from ''consequi'' (to follow closely)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"con-","root":"sequ","suffix":"-ence"},"relatedWords":["consequential","consequently","inconsequential"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":41,"contextSentence":"Yeah, maybe there''ll be consequences if you don''t work late."},{"word":"cosmic","phonetic":"/ˈkɑːzmɪk/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"relating to the universe; infinitely great","meaningKo":"우주적인, 광대한","etymology":"From Greek ''kosmikos'', from ''kosmos'' (world, universe)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"cosm-","suffix":"-ic"},"relatedWords":["cosmos","cosmology","cosmopolitan"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":50,"contextSentence":"But expecting success to fall into your lap like some sort of cosmic reward."},{"word":"muster","phonetic":"/ˈmʌstər/","partOfSpeech":"verb","definition":"to summon up or gather (a feeling, attitude, or response)","meaningKo":"끌어모으다, 소집하다","etymology":"From Old French ''mostrer'' (to show), from Latin ''monstrare''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"muster","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["mustering","muster up"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":55,"contextSentence":"Where you need all the mental strength that you can muster."},{"word":"therapist","phonetic":"/ˈθɛrəpɪst/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"a person skilled in a particular kind of therapy","meaningKo":"치료사, 상담사","etymology":"From Greek ''therapeutes'' (one who serves or attends)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"therap-","suffix":"-ist"},"relatedWords":["therapy","therapeutic","therapize"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":57,"contextSentence":"I landed my first big job as a therapist."},{"word":"unresponsive","phonetic":"/ˌʌnrɪˈspɑːnsɪv/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"not responding to stimulation or treatment; unconscious","meaningKo":"반응이 없는, 의식이 없는","etymology":"From ''un-'' (not) + ''responsive'' (Latin ''respondere'')","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"un-","root":"respons-","suffix":"-ive"},"relatedWords":["responsive","response","respond"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":62,"contextSentence":"She said that our mother was found unresponsive."},{"word":"aneurysm","phonetic":"/ˈænjʊrɪzəm/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"a bulge in a blood vessel caused by weakness in the vessel wall","meaningKo":"동맥류","etymology":"From Greek ''aneurysma'' (a widening), from ''aneurynein'' (to dilate)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"an-","root":"eurys-","suffix":"-m"},"relatedWords":["aneurysmal"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":66,"contextSentence":"Doctors explained she''d had a brain aneurysm."},{"word":"devastating","phonetic":"/ˈdɛvəsteɪtɪŋ/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"causing severe shock, distress, or destruction","meaningKo":"충격적인, 엄청난 타격을 주는","etymology":"From Latin ''devastare'' (to lay waste), from ''de-'' + ''vastare'' (to empty)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"de-","root":"vast-","suffix":"-ating"},"relatedWords":["devastate","devastation","devastated"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":69,"contextSentence":"That news was devastating to me."},{"word":"intellectual","phonetic":"/ˌɪntəˈlɛktʃuəl/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"relating to the intellect; involving mental rather than emotional processes","meaningKo":"지적인, 지성의","etymology":"From Latin ''intellectualis'', from ''intellegere'' (to understand)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"intel-","root":"lect-","suffix":"-ual"},"relatedWords":["intellect","intellectually","intelligence"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":70,"contextSentence":"I knew on an intellectual level how to go through grief."},{"word":"grief","phonetic":"/ɡriːf/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"deep sorrow, especially caused by someone''s death","meaningKo":"깊은 슬픔, 비탄","etymology":"From Old French ''grief'' (hardship, suffering), from ''grever'' (to burden)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"grief","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["grieve","grievance","grieving"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":70,"contextSentence":"I knew on an intellectual level how to go through grief."},{"word":"coincidentally","phonetic":"/koʊˌɪnsɪˈdɛntəli/","partOfSpeech":"adverb","definition":"in a way that results from coincidence; by chance","meaningKo":"우연히, 공교롭게도","etymology":"From Latin ''co-'' (together) + ''incidere'' (to fall upon)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"co-","root":"incid-","suffix":"-ent-ally"},"relatedWords":["coincidence","coincide","coincidental"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":75,"contextSentence":"Coincidentally, it was being played at the same auditorium where I had last seen my mother."},{"word":"understatement","phonetic":"/ˈʌndərsteɪtmənt/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"a statement that represents something as less than it is","meaningKo":"과소표현, 절제된 표현","etymology":"From ''under-'' + ''statement''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"under-","root":"state","suffix":"-ment"},"relatedWords":["understate","overstatement","overstate"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":93,"contextSentence":"To describe that as a painful period in my life feels like an understatement."},{"word":"terminal","phonetic":"/ˈtɜːrmɪnəl/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"predicted to lead to death; incurable","meaningKo":"말기의, 불치의","etymology":"From Latin ''terminalis'', from ''terminus'' (end, boundary)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"termin-","suffix":"-al"},"relatedWords":["terminate","terminal","terminology"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":103,"contextSentence":"I got the news that Steve''s dad had terminal cancer."},{"word":"gratitude","phonetic":"/ˈɡrætɪtuːd/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"the quality of being thankful; readiness to show appreciation","meaningKo":"감사, 고마움","etymology":"From Medieval Latin ''gratitudo'', from Latin ''gratus'' (thankful, pleasing)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"grat-","suffix":"-itude"},"relatedWords":["grateful","gratify","ingratitude"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":117,"contextSentence":"You need good habits like practicing gratitude."},{"word":"resenting","phonetic":"/rɪˈzɛntɪŋ/","partOfSpeech":"verb","definition":"feeling bitterness or indignation toward someone or something","meaningKo":"원망하는, 분개하는","etymology":"From French ''ressentir'' (to feel strongly), from Latin ''re-'' + ''sentire'' (to feel)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"re-","root":"sent-","suffix":"-ing"},"relatedWords":["resentment","resentful","resent"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":118,"contextSentence":"You also have to give up bad habits like resenting somebody else''s success."},{"word":"liberating","phonetic":"/ˈlɪbəreɪtɪŋ/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"freeing from restriction or oppression","meaningKo":"해방감을 주는, 자유롭게 하는","etymology":"From Latin ''liberare'' (to set free), from ''liber'' (free)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"liber-","suffix":"-ating"},"relatedWords":["liberate","liberation","liberty"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":142,"contextSentence":"And that can be liberating."},{"word":"doomed","phonetic":"/duːmd/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"destined to an unhappy or unfortunate fate","meaningKo":"운명에 처한, 파멸할","etymology":"From Old English ''dōm'' (judgment, fate)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"doom","suffix":"-ed"},"relatedWords":["doom","doomsday","doomful"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":149,"contextSentence":"So he just believed he was doomed."},{"word":"complications","phonetic":"/ˌkɑːmplɪˈkeɪʃənz/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"secondary diseases or conditions that worsen an existing one","meaningKo":"합병증, 복잡한 사태","etymology":"From Latin ''complicatio'', from ''complicare'' (to fold together)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"com-","root":"plic-","suffix":"-ation"},"relatedWords":["complicate","complicated","complexity"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":148,"contextSentence":"His mother had died from complications of diabetes at a young age."},{"word":"diabetic","phonetic":"/ˌdaɪəˈbɛtɪk/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"having or relating to diabetes","meaningKo":"당뇨병의, 당뇨가 있는","etymology":"From Greek ''diabētikos'', from ''diabētēs'' (siphon, one who straddles)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"dia-","root":"bet-","suffix":"-ic"},"relatedWords":["diabetes","prediabetic"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":146,"contextSentence":"I once worked with this man who had been diabetic for years."}]'::jsonb, '[{"pattern":"Relative Clause with ''whose''","explanation":"Used to show possession in a relative clause, connecting a noun to additional information about something belonging to it.","explanationKo":"''whose''는 관계절에서 소유를 나타내며, 명사와 그에 속하는 것에 대한 추가 정보를 연결합니다. ''~의''라는 의미를 가집니다.","examples":["I have a Facebook friend whose life seems perfect.","My mother, who used to wake up in the morning saying..."],"segmentIndex":0},{"pattern":"Present Perfect Progressive (have/has been + -ing)","explanation":"Expresses an action that started in the past and continues to the present, emphasizing the duration or ongoing nature of the action.","explanationKo":"과거에 시작되어 현재까지 계속되는 동작을 표현하며, 동작의 지속 기간이나 진행 중인 성격을 강조합니다.","examples":["She''s always posting about how blessed she is.","His blood sugar had gotten so high lately that it was starting to affect his vision."],"segmentIndex":5},{"pattern":"Rhetorical Questions","explanation":"Questions asked not to get an answer but to make a point or engage the audience. Common in persuasive speech and presentations.","explanationKo":"답을 구하기 위해서가 아니라 요점을 전달하거나 청중을 참여시키기 위해 하는 질문입니다. 설득력 있는 연설과 프레젠테이션에서 흔합니다.","examples":["How many of you have a friend kind of like that?","How could it be hurting me?","How do you train your brain to think differently?"],"segmentIndex":9},{"pattern":"Parallel Structure (keeps you... keeps you... keeps you...)","explanation":"Using the same grammatical form for items in a list or series to create rhythm, clarity, and emphasis.","explanationKo":"목록이나 일련의 항목에 같은 문법적 형태를 사용하여 리듬감, 명확성, 강조를 만드는 기법입니다.","examples":["Keeps you focused on the problem, keeps you from finding a solution.","Feeling sad or hurt or angry or scared, those things are all uncomfortable."],"segmentIndex":32},{"pattern":"Conditional with ''if'' (Second Conditional)","explanation":"Used to talk about hypothetical situations and their consequences. Structure: If + past simple, would + base verb.","explanationKo":"가정적인 상황과 그 결과에 대해 말할 때 사용합니다. 구조: If + 과거 단순, would + 동사 원형.","examples":["If you wanted to be physically strong, you''d need to go to the gym and lift weights.","If you really wanted to see results, you''d also have to give up eating junk food."],"segmentIndex":115},{"pattern":"Modal Verb ''must'' for Strong Deduction","explanation":"Using ''must'' to express a strong belief or logical conclusion about something based on evidence.","explanationKo":"''must''를 사용하여 증거에 기반한 강한 믿음이나 논리적 결론을 표현합니다.","examples":["I swear that they must take a professional photographer along with them.","Where you need all the mental strength that you can muster."],"segmentIndex":3},{"pattern":"Past Perfect (had + past participle)","explanation":"Used to show that one action was completed before another action in the past, establishing a clear sequence of events.","explanationKo":"과거의 다른 동작 이전에 하나의 동작이 완료되었음을 보여주기 위해 사용되며, 명확한 사건 순서를 설정합니다.","examples":["He''d had a heart attack.","I hadn''t been back there since.","His blood sugar had gotten so high."],"segmentIndex":89},{"pattern":"Comparative Structures (more... than / less... than)","explanation":"Used to compare two things, showing that one has more or less of a quality than the other.","explanationKo":"두 가지를 비교하는 데 사용되며, 하나가 다른 것보다 어떤 특성이 더 많거나 적음을 보여줍니다.","examples":["He could see the TV just a little bit more clearly than he had before.","A snack with a little less sugar."],"segmentIndex":166},{"pattern":"''No matter'' + WH-word (Concessive Clause)","explanation":"Used to express that something is true regardless of the condition. Equivalent to ''it doesn''t matter what/where/how...''","explanationKo":"조건에 상관없이 어떤 것이 사실임을 표현할 때 사용합니다. ''무엇이든/어디든/어떻게든 상관없이''와 같은 의미입니다.","examples":["No matter where they go or what they do.","No matter how often that happens, it''ll hold you back.","No matter how small they might seem."],"segmentIndex":4},{"pattern":"Gerund as Subject","explanation":"Using the -ing form of a verb as the subject of a sentence to discuss activities or concepts in general terms.","explanationKo":"동사의 -ing 형태를 문장의 주어로 사용하여 활동이나 개념을 일반적으로 논의합니다.","examples":["Feeling sad or hurt or angry or scared, those things are all uncomfortable.","Expecting success to fall into your lap will only lead to disappointment."],"segmentIndex":124},{"pattern":"Reported Speech (said that...)","explanation":"Reporting what someone else said using ''said/told/explained + that clause'', often with a shift in tense.","explanationKo":"''said/told/explained + that절''을 사용하여 다른 사람이 말한 것을 전달하며, 종종 시제가 바뀝니다.","examples":["She said that our mother was found unresponsive.","He said, I''ll give up my two liter a day Pepsi habit.","He said, I could trade in my nightly bowl of ice cream."],"segmentIndex":62},{"pattern":"Correlative Conjunctions (not just... but also)","explanation":"Paired conjunctions used to join grammatically equal elements and add emphasis to the relationship between them.","explanationKo":"문법적으로 동등한 요소를 연결하고 그 관계를 강조하기 위해 사용되는 짝을 이루는 접속사입니다.","examples":["Not just to create good habits in my life, but to get rid of those small habits.","Not only did he lose weight, but one day he noticed he could see the TV."],"segmentIndex":96}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"linking","originalText":"seems perfect","phonetic":"/siːmz‿pɜːrfɪkt/","koreanPhonetic":"심즈 퍼펙트","explanation":"The final /z/ of ''seems'' links smoothly into the initial /p/ of ''perfect'', creating a continuous flow.","explanationKo":"''seems''의 마지막 /z/ 소리가 ''perfect''의 첫 /p/ 소리와 부드럽게 연결되어 연속적인 흐름을 만듭니다.","practiceGuide":"''심즈퍼펙트''처럼 끊김 없이 한 단어처럼 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":0},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"kind of","phonetic":"/ˈkaɪndə/","koreanPhonetic":"카인다","explanation":"''Kind of'' is commonly reduced to ''kinda'' in casual speech, with the ''of'' becoming a weak schwa sound.","explanationKo":"''Kind of''는 일상 대화에서 흔히 ''kinda''로 줄여 발음하며, ''of''가 약한 슈와 소리로 됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''카인다''로 발음하세요. ''of''를 또렷이 말하지 않고 약하게 ''다''처럼 붙여서 말합니다.","segmentIndex":9},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"don''t like","phonetic":"/doʊn(t)laɪk/","koreanPhonetic":"돈 라이크","explanation":"The /t/ at the end of ''don''t'' is often reduced or dropped before the /l/ in ''like'', creating a smoother transition.","explanationKo":"''don''t''의 끝 /t/ 소리가 ''like''의 /l/ 앞에서 줄어들거나 생략되어 더 부드러운 전환이 됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''돈라이크''처럼 t를 거의 발음하지 않고 부드럽게 연결하세요.","segmentIndex":10},{"type":"linking","originalText":"costs us","phonetic":"/kɑːsts‿ʌs/","koreanPhonetic":"카스츠어스","explanation":"The final /s/ cluster of ''costs'' links into the vowel /ʌ/ of ''us'', with the consonant cluster simplifying in fast speech.","explanationKo":"''costs''의 마지막 /s/ 자음군이 ''us''의 모음 /ʌ/에 연결되며, 빠른 발화에서 자음군이 단순화됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''카스츠''와 ''어스'' 사이에 끊김 없이 연결하세요.","segmentIndex":12},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"going to","phonetic":"/ˈɡɒnə/","koreanPhonetic":"고나","explanation":"''Going to'' is frequently reduced to ''gonna'' in spoken English, especially before another verb.","explanationKo":"''Going to''는 구어 영어에서, 특히 다른 동사 앞에서 ''gonna''로 자주 줄여 발음됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''고나''로 발음하세요. ''going to''를 따로따로 말하지 말고 ''고나''로 합쳐서 말합니다.","segmentIndex":54},{"type":"assimilation","originalText":"jumped in","phonetic":"/dʒʌmpt‿ɪn/","koreanPhonetic":"점프틴","explanation":"The final /d/ of ''jumped'' assimilates to /t/ due to the voiceless preceding consonant, and then links into ''in''.","explanationKo":"''jumped''의 마지막 /d/가 앞의 무성 자음으로 인해 /t/로 동화되고, ''in''으로 연결됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''점프틴''처럼 발음하세요. jumped의 -ed가 /t/로 발음됩니다.","segmentIndex":63},{"type":"elision","originalText":"didn''t have","phonetic":"/ˈdɪdn̩(t)hæv/","koreanPhonetic":"디든 해브","explanation":"The /t/ at the end of ''didn''t'' is often elided (dropped) before the /h/ of ''have'', making the phrase flow more smoothly.","explanationKo":"''didn''t''의 끝 /t/가 ''have''의 /h/ 앞에서 생략(탈락)되어 문장이 더 부드럽게 흐릅니다.","practiceGuide":"''디든해브''처럼 발음하세요. t를 생략하고 바로 ''have''로 넘어갑니다.","segmentIndex":65},{"type":"linking","originalText":"figure it out","phonetic":"/ˈfɪɡjər‿ɪt‿aʊt/","koreanPhonetic":"피겨릿 아웃","explanation":"The /r/ of ''figure'' links to the vowel /ɪ/ of ''it'', and then ''it'' links to ''out'' through consonant-vowel linking.","explanationKo":"''figure''의 /r/이 ''it''의 모음 /ɪ/에 연결되고, ''it''이 자음-모음 연결을 통해 ''out''에 연결됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''피겨릿아웃''처럼 세 단어를 부드럽게 연결해서 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":56},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"want to","phonetic":"/ˈwɒnə/","koreanPhonetic":"워나","explanation":"''Want to'' is commonly reduced to ''wanna'' in casual speech, merging the two words.","explanationKo":"''Want to''는 일상 대화에서 ''wanna''로 흔히 줄여 발음되며, 두 단어가 합쳐집니다.","practiceGuide":"''워나''로 발음하세요. ''to''를 따로 발음하지 말고 ''want''에 붙여서 말합니다.","segmentIndex":13},{"type":"linking","originalText":"hold you back","phonetic":"/hoʊld‿juː‿bæk/","koreanPhonetic":"홀쥬 백","explanation":"The /d/ of ''hold'' links into the /j/ of ''you'', and ''you'' connects to ''back'', creating a smooth three-word chain.","explanationKo":"''hold''의 /d/가 ''you''의 /j/에 연결되고, ''you''가 ''back''에 연결되어 부드러운 세 단어 체인을 만듭니다.","practiceGuide":"''홀쥬백''처럼 세 단어를 하나로 연결하여 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":25},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"have to","phonetic":"/ˈhæftə/","koreanPhonetic":"해프타","explanation":"''Have to'' is reduced to ''hafta'' in fast speech, with the /v/ becoming /f/ and ''to'' becoming /tə/.","explanationKo":"''Have to''는 빠른 발화에서 ''hafta''로 줄여지며, /v/가 /f/로 바뀌고 ''to''가 /tə/가 됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''해프타''로 발음하세요. ''have''의 v가 f로 변하고 ''to''가 약하게 발음됩니다.","segmentIndex":30},{"type":"assimilation","originalText":"used to","phonetic":"/ˈjuːstə/","koreanPhonetic":"유스타","explanation":"The /d/ of ''used'' assimilates to /t/ when followed by ''to'', and ''to'' reduces to /tə/.","explanationKo":"''used''의 /d/가 ''to'' 앞에서 /t/로 동화되고, ''to''는 /tə/로 축소됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''유스타''로 발음하세요. ''d''가 ''t''로 바뀌고 ''to''가 약하게 발음됩니다.","segmentIndex":67},{"type":"elision","originalText":"just looks","phonetic":"/dʒʌs(t)lʊks/","koreanPhonetic":"저스 룩스","explanation":"The /t/ at the end of ''just'' is often dropped before the /l/ of ''looks'' in connected speech.","explanationKo":"연결 발화에서 ''just''의 마지막 /t/가 ''looks''의 /l/ 앞에서 종종 탈락합니다.","practiceGuide":"''저스룩스''처럼 발음하세요. ''just''의 t를 생략하고 바로 ''looks''로 연결합니다.","segmentIndex":5},{"type":"linking","originalText":"give up","phonetic":"/ɡɪv‿ʌp/","koreanPhonetic":"기버프","explanation":"The final /v/ of ''give'' links directly to the initial vowel /ʌ/ of ''up'', creating a consonant-to-vowel link.","explanationKo":"''give''의 마지막 /v/가 ''up''의 첫 모음 /ʌ/에 직접 연결되어 자음-모음 연결을 만듭니다.","practiceGuide":"''기버프''처럼 발음하세요. ''give''와 ''up''을 끊지 말고 연결합니다.","segmentIndex":51},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"a lot of","phonetic":"/əˈlɒtə/","koreanPhonetic":"어라러","explanation":"''A lot of'' is reduced in casual speech with the ''t'' becoming a flap and ''of'' reducing to /ə/.","explanationKo":"''A lot of''는 일상 대화에서 축소되며, ''t''가 플랩음이 되고 ''of''가 /ə/로 줄어듭니다.","practiceGuide":"''어라러''처럼 발음하세요. ''t''를 가볍게 치고 ''of''를 약하게 발음합니다.","segmentIndex":114}]'::jsonb, '{"sections":[{"section":"Introduction - The Facebook Friend","startSegment":0,"endSegment":8,"summary":"Amy opens with a relatable story about a Facebook friend whose life appears perfect, establishing an emotional connection with the audience through shared experience.","summaryKo":"에이미는 완벽한 삶을 사는 것처럼 보이는 페이스북 친구에 대한 공감할 수 있는 이야기로 시작하며, 공유된 경험을 통해 청중과 감정적 연결을 설정합니다.","keyPoints":["완벽해 보이는 삶을 사는 페이스북 친구 소개","소셜 미디어에서의 비교 심리를 활용한 청중 공감 형성"]},{"section":"Audience Engagement - The Cost of Bad Habits","startSegment":9,"endSegment":23,"summary":"She engages the audience with rhetorical questions and reveals that envying friends on Facebook leads to depression, introducing the concept that our bad mental habits cost us.","summaryKo":"수사적 질문으로 청중을 참여시키고, 페이스북에서 친구를 부러워하는 것이 우울증으로 이어진다는 것을 밝히며, 나쁜 정신적 습관이 대가를 치르게 한다는 개념을 소개합니다.","keyPoints":["페이스북 질투가 실제로 우울증을 유발한다는 연구 결과","작아 보이는 생각 습관이 정신적 강인함을 갉아먹음"]},{"section":"Three Destructive Beliefs - Part 1: About Ourselves","startSegment":24,"endSegment":35,"summary":"Amy introduces the three types of destructive beliefs, starting with unhealthy beliefs about ourselves, specifically self-pity and how it keeps us stuck.","summaryKo":"에이미는 세 가지 유형의 파괴적인 믿음을 소개하며, 자기 자신에 대한 건강하지 못한 믿음, 특히 자기연민과 그것이 어떻게 우리를 막히게 하는지 시작합니다.","keyPoints":["자기연민은 불행을 과장하는 것","자기연민 파티를 열면 문제 해결에서 멀어짐"]},{"section":"Three Destructive Beliefs - Part 2: About Others","startSegment":36,"endSegment":45,"summary":"The second destructive belief is about others - thinking they can control us. She illustrates how phrases like ''I have to'' and ''she drives me crazy'' give away our power.","summaryKo":"두 번째 파괴적인 믿음은 다른 사람들에 대한 것으로, 그들이 우리를 통제할 수 있다고 생각하는 것입니다. ''해야 해''와 ''미치게 만들어'' 같은 표현이 우리의 권한을 내어주는 것을 설명합니다.","keyPoints":["''해야 한다''는 표현이 자신의 권한을 포기하는 것","다른 사람에 대한 반응은 항상 자신의 선택"]},{"section":"Three Destructive Beliefs - Part 3: About the World","startSegment":46,"endSegment":55,"summary":"The third destructive belief is that the world owes us something. Amy argues that expecting cosmic rewards for hard work leads to disappointment.","summaryKo":"세 번째 파괴적인 믿음은 세상이 우리에게 무언가 빚지고 있다는 것입니다. 열심히 일한 대가로 우주적 보상을 기대하는 것은 실망만 가져온다고 주장합니다.","keyPoints":["성공이 자동으로 따라올 거라는 기대는 실망을 초래","정신적 강인함을 모두 끌어모아야 할 때가 올 것"]},{"section":"Personal Story - Loss of Mother","startSegment":56,"endSegment":72,"summary":"Amy shares her deeply personal story of losing her mother to a brain aneurysm at age 51, when Amy was just 23, and the long healing process.","summaryKo":"에이미는 23살 때 51세의 어머니를 뇌동맥류로 잃은 매우 개인적인 이야기와 긴 치유 과정을 공유합니다.","keyPoints":["23살에 뇌동맥류로 51세 어머니를 잃음","아는 것과 실천하는 것은 다르다는 깨달음"]},{"section":"Personal Story - Loss of Husband","startSegment":73,"endSegment":93,"summary":"On the three-year anniversary of her mother''s death, Amy''s husband Lincoln suddenly collapses and dies of a heart attack at age 26, leaving her a young widow.","summaryKo":"어머니 사망 3주년에 남편 링컨이 26세에 갑작스런 심장마비로 사망하여, 젊은 과부가 됩니다.","keyPoints":["어머니 기일에 남편의 갑작스러운 심장마비 사망","26세 과부가 되어 엄마도 없는 상황"]},{"section":"The Turning Point - Mental Strength List","startSegment":94,"endSegment":113,"summary":"Amy explains how she realized good habits alone aren''t enough during tough times. After remarrying and facing yet another loss, she wrote her famous list of things mentally strong people don''t do.","summaryKo":"에이미는 힘든 시기에 좋은 습관만으로는 충분하지 않다는 것을 깨달았다고 설명합니다. 재혼 후 또 다른 상실에 직면하여 정신적으로 강한 사람들이 하지 않는 것들의 유명한 목록을 작성했습니다.","keyPoints":["나쁜 습관 하나 둘이면 충분히 발목을 잡을 수 있음","정신적으로 강한 사람들이 하지 않는 것 목록 작성"]},{"section":"Core Message - Mental vs Physical Strength Analogy","startSegment":114,"endSegment":121,"summary":"Amy draws a powerful analogy between mental and physical strength, arguing that just as physical fitness requires both exercise and giving up junk food, mental strength requires good habits and eliminating bad ones.","summaryKo":"에이미는 정신적 강인함과 신체적 강인함 사이의 강력한 비유를 그립니다. 체력이 운동과 정크 푸드 포기를 모두 필요로 하듯이, 정신적 강인함도 좋은 습관과 나쁜 습관 제거를 필요로 한다고 주장합니다.","keyPoints":["정신적 강인함은 신체적 강인함과 같은 원리","좋은 습관 형성과 나쁜 습관 제거 모두 필요"]},{"section":"Solutions - Countering Unhealthy Beliefs","startSegment":122,"endSegment":144,"summary":"Amy provides practical solutions for each type of destructive belief: face uncomfortable emotions instead of avoiding them, stop comparing yourself to others, and accept that life isn''t fair.","summaryKo":"에이미는 각 유형의 파괴적인 믿음에 대한 실질적인 해결책을 제공합니다: 불편한 감정을 피하는 대신 직면하기, 다른 사람과 비교를 멈추기, 인생이 공평하지 않다는 것 받아들이기.","keyPoints":["불편한 감정을 겪어야 극복할 수 있음","자신을 비교할 유일한 대상은 어제의 나","인생의 불공평함을 받아들이면 해방감을 얻음"]},{"section":"Case Study - The Diabetic Man","startSegment":145,"endSegment":175,"summary":"Amy illustrates her points with a compelling case study of a diabetic man who believed he was doomed, but transformed his life through one small change at a time.","summaryKo":"에이미는 운명이라고 믿었지만 한 번에 하나의 작은 변화를 통해 삶을 변화시킨 당뇨병 남성의 설득력 있는 사례 연구로 자신의 주장을 설명합니다.","keyPoints":["작은 변화 하나가 큰 변화의 시작이 될 수 있음","자신의 세상을 바꿀 수 있다고 믿는 것의 중요성","펩시 하나를 바꾸는 것에서 시작하여 운전면허 회복까지"]},{"section":"Conclusion - Call to Action","startSegment":176,"endSegment":179,"summary":"Amy concludes by inviting the audience to identify their own bad mental habits and take one small step today.","summaryKo":"에이미는 청중에게 자신의 나쁜 정신적 습관을 파악하고 오늘 하나의 작은 발걸음을 내딛으라고 권하며 마무리합니다.","keyPoints":["자신을 막는 나쁜 정신적 습관 파악하기","오늘 바로 지금 하나의 작은 발걸음 내딛기"]}],"signalExpressions":[{"expression":"So I have a Facebook friend whose life seems perfect.","type":"hook","segmentIndex":0,"role":"개인적 이야기로 시작하여 청중의 관심을 즉시 끌어들이는 도입 훅"},{"expression":"And that''s what I want to talk to you about today","type":"transition","segmentIndex":13,"role":"일화에서 주제 제시로 전환하는 신호 표현"},{"expression":"There''s three kinds of destructive beliefs","type":"transition","segmentIndex":24,"role":"핵심 내용의 구조를 미리 알려주는 로드맵 제시"},{"expression":"The first one is unhealthy beliefs about ourselves.","type":"transition","segmentIndex":26,"role":"첫 번째 핵심 포인트를 명확히 도입하는 순서 표현"},{"expression":"The second type of destructive belief that holds us back","type":"transition","segmentIndex":36,"role":"두 번째 핵심 포인트로 전환하는 순서 표현"},{"expression":"The third type of unhealthy belief that holds us back","type":"transition","segmentIndex":46,"role":"세 번째 핵심 포인트로 전환하는 순서 표현"},{"expression":"When I was 23 years old, I thought I had life all figured out.","type":"hook","segmentIndex":56,"role":"개인적 이야기의 시작을 알리는 서사적 전환"},{"expression":"That all changed for me one day","type":"transition","segmentIndex":61,"role":"극적인 전환점을 알리는 서사적 신호"},{"expression":"My journey taught me that the secret to being mentally strong","type":"transition","segmentIndex":112,"role":"개인적 이야기에서 핵심 교훈으로 전환"},{"expression":"Mental strength is a lot like physical strength.","type":"transition","segmentIndex":114,"role":"비유를 사용한 핵심 메시지 전달의 시작"},{"expression":"So, how do you train your brain to think differently?","type":"transition","segmentIndex":120,"role":"문제에서 해결책으로 전환하는 수사적 질문"},{"expression":"I once worked with this man who had been diabetic for years.","type":"hook","segmentIndex":146,"role":"사례 연구를 도입하는 서사적 시작"},{"expression":"And it all started with just one small step.","type":"conclusion","segmentIndex":175,"role":"사례 연구를 마무리하며 핵심 메시지를 강조"},{"expression":"So I invite you to consider what bad mental habits are holding you back.","type":"conclusion","segmentIndex":176,"role":"청중에게 직접 행동을 촉구하는 결론 시작"},{"expression":"And what''s one small step that you could take today, right here, right now?","type":"conclusion","segmentIndex":178,"role":"즉각적인 행동을 촉구하며 강력하게 마무리하는 최종 질문"}]}'::jsonb
)
