 brain, involved in complex cognitive behavior, decision-making, and moderating social behavior","meaningKo":"전전두엽 피질","etymology":"From Latin ''prae'' (before) + ''frons'' (forehead) + ''cortex'' (bark, outer layer)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"pre- (앞의)","root":"frontal (이마의) + cortex (피질)","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["cortex","frontal","prefrontal"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":70,"contextSentence":"Now, the prefrontal cortex,"},{"word":"cognitive control","phonetic":"/ˈkɒɡnətɪv kənˈtroʊl/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"The mental processes that regulate thoughts and actions in accordance with internally maintained goals","meaningKo":"인지적 통제","etymology":"From Latin ''cognoscere'' (to know) + ''contrarotulus'' (counter-roll, check)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"cognit- (인식) + control (통제)","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["cognition","self-control","executive function"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":75,"contextSentence":"We call this cognitive control. We''re using cognition to control our behavior."},{"word":"visceral","phonetic":"/ˈvɪsərəl/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"Relating to deep inward feelings rather than to the intellect; felt in or as if in the internal organs of the body","meaningKo":"본능적인, 내장의, 직감적인","etymology":"From Latin ''viscera'' meaning ''internal organs''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"viscer- (내장)","suffix":"-al (형용사형 접미사)"},"relatedWords":["viscerally","viscera","eviscerate"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":89,"contextSentence":"Becoming disenchanted on a visceral level,"},{"word":"paradox","phonetic":"/ˈpærədɒks/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"A seemingly contradictory statement or situation that may nonetheless be true","meaningKo":"역설, 모순","etymology":"From Greek ''paradoxos'' meaning ''contrary to expectation''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"para- (반대의, 넘어서)","root":"dox (생각, 의견)","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["paradoxical","paradoxically","orthodox"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":94,"contextSentence":"The paradox here is that mindfulness is just about being really interested"},{"word":"craving","phonetic":"/ˈkreɪvɪŋ/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"A powerful desire or longing for something","meaningKo":"갈망, 열망","etymology":"From Old English ''crafian'' meaning ''to demand, beg''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"crave (갈망하다)","suffix":"-ing (명사형 접미사)"},"relatedWords":["crave","craved","yearning"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":98,"contextSentence":"Rather than trying to make unpleasant cravings go away"},{"word":"restlessness","phonetic":"/ˈrestləsnəs/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"The inability to rest or relax as a result of anxiety or boredom","meaningKo":"안절부절못함, 초조함","etymology":"From Old English ''rest'' + ''-less'' + ''-ness''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"rest (휴식)","suffix":"-lessness (없는 상태)"},"relatedWords":["restless","rest","unrest"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":103,"contextSentence":"Oh, there''s tightness, there''s tension, there''s restlessness,"},{"word":"clobbered","phonetic":"/ˈklɒbərd/","partOfSpeech":"verb (past tense)","definition":"To hit hard; to defeat or overwhelm decisively","meaningKo":"세게 때리다, 압도당하다","etymology":"Origin uncertain, possibly from British dialect, early 20th century","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"clobber (세게 때리다)","suffix":"-ed (과거형 접미사)"},"relatedWords":["overwhelm","batter","pummel"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":106,"contextSentence":"rather than getting clobbered by this huge, scary craving that we choke on."},{"word":"reactive","phonetic":"/riˈæktɪv/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"Acting in response to a situation rather than creating or controlling it; tending to react impulsively","meaningKo":"반응적인, 수동적인","etymology":"From Latin ''re-'' (back) + ''agere'' (to do, act)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"re- (다시, 되돌아)","root":"act (행동하다)","suffix":"-ive (형용사형 접미사)"},"relatedWords":["reaction","react","proactive"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":108,"contextSentence":"We step out of our old fear-based reactive habit patterns,"},{"word":"self-referential","phonetic":"/ˌself rɛfəˈrɛnʃəl/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"Referring to or directed toward oneself; relating to one''s own thoughts and identity","meaningKo":"자기 참조적인","etymology":"From ''self'' + Latin ''referre'' (to carry back, relate)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"self- (자기)","root":"refer (언급하다, 참조하다)","suffix":"-ential (형용사형 접미사)"},"relatedWords":["reference","referential","self-aware"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":116,"contextSentence":"We found that parts of a neural network of self-referential processing"},{"word":"default mode network","phonetic":"/dɪˈfɔːlt moʊd ˈnetwɜːrk/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"A large-scale brain network primarily composed of the medial prefrontal cortex and posterior cingulate cortex, active when a person is not focused on the outside world","meaningKo":"디폴트 모드 네트워크 (기본 모드 회로망)","etymology":"Modern neuroscience term; ''default'' from Old French ''defaute'' (failure) + ''mode'' + ''network''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"default (기본값) + mode (양식) + network (회로망)","suffix":null},"relatedWords":["neural network","resting state","brain connectivity"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":117,"contextSentence":"Called the default mode network were at play."},{"word":"posterior cingulate cortex","phonetic":"/pɒˈstɪəriər ˈsɪŋɡjʊlət ˈkɔːrteks/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"A brain region that is a central node of the default mode network, involved in internally directed cognition","meaningKo":"후대상피질","etymology":"From Latin ''posterior'' (coming after) + ''cingulum'' (belt, girdle) + ''cortex'' (bark)","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"post- (뒤의)","root":"cingul- (띠) + cortex (피질)","suffix":"-ate (형용사형 접미사)"},"relatedWords":["anterior cingulate","cortex","cingulate gyrus"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":118,"contextSentence":"Called the posterior cingulate cortex is activated not necessarily by craving itself,"},{"word":"morbidity","phonetic":"/mɔːrˈbɪdɪti/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"The condition of suffering from a disease or medical condition; the rate of disease in a population","meaningKo":"이환율, 유병률","etymology":"From Latin ''morbidus'' meaning ''sickly, diseased''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"morb- (병, 질병)","suffix":"-idity (상태를 나타내는 명사 접미사)"},"relatedWords":["morbid","mortality","comorbidity"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":44,"contextSentence":"Obesity and smoking are among the leading preventable causes of morbidity and mortality in the world."},{"word":"mortality","phonetic":"/mɔːrˈtælɪti/","partOfSpeech":"noun","definition":"The state of being subject to death; the death rate in a population","meaningKo":"사망률","etymology":"From Latin ''mortalis'' meaning ''subject to death''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":null,"root":"mort- (죽음)","suffix":"-ality (상태를 나타내는 명사 접미사)"},"relatedWords":["mortal","immortal","mortuary"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":45,"contextSentence":"Of morbidity and mortality in the world."},{"word":"compulsively","phonetic":"/kəmˈpʌlsɪvli/","partOfSpeech":"adverb","definition":"In a way that results from or relates to an irresistible urge, especially one that is against one''s conscious wishes","meaningKo":"강박적으로","etymology":"From Latin ''compulsus'' meaning ''driven together, forced''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"com- (함께)","root":"puls- (밀다, 몰다)","suffix":"-ively (부사형 접미사)"},"relatedWords":["compulsive","compulsion","impulse"],"isEssential":true,"segmentIndex":135,"contextSentence":"Or maybe to compulsively respond to that text message when you''re driving,"},{"word":"perpetuate","phonetic":"/pərˈpetʃueɪt/","partOfSpeech":"verb","definition":"To make something continue indefinitely; to preserve from extinction or oblivion","meaningKo":"영속시키다, 지속시키다","etymology":"From Latin ''perpetuare'' meaning ''to make permanent''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"per- (완전히, 끝까지)","root":"petu- (추구하다)","suffix":"-ate (동사형 접미사)"},"relatedWords":["perpetual","perpetually","perpetuity"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":138,"contextSentence":"It will just be another chance to perpetuate"},{"word":"exhausting","phonetic":"/ɪɡˈzɔːstɪŋ/","partOfSpeech":"adjective","definition":"Making one feel very tired; extremely tiring","meaningKo":"극도로 피곤하게 하는, 녹초가 되는","etymology":"From Latin ''exhaurire'' meaning ''to draw out completely''","rootBreakdown":{"prefix":"ex- (밖으로, 완전히)","root":"haust- (끌어내다)","suffix":"-ing (형용사형 접미사)"},"relatedWords":["exhaust","exhaustion","exhaustive"],"isEssential":false,"segmentIndex":7,"contextSentence":"Actually, it was exhausting."}]'::jsonb, '[{"pattern":"Past Habitual with ''would'' (contracted ''d)","explanation":"The contracted form ''I''d'' (I would) is used to describe repeated past actions or habits. This is common in storytelling to describe what someone used to do regularly.","explanationKo":"''I''d'' (I would의 축약형)는 과거에 반복적으로 했던 행동이나 습관을 설명할 때 사용됩니다. 이야기를 할 때 과거의 일상적 행동을 묘사하는 데 흔히 쓰입니다.","examples":["Yet I''d sit on these silent retreats,","I''d take naps every chance I got because it was really hard work."],"segmentIndex":4},{"pattern":"It turns out that...","explanation":"This expression is used to introduce a surprising or previously unknown fact. It signals that the speaker is about to reveal something that may change the listener''s understanding of a situation.","explanationKo":"''It turns out that...''은 놀랍거나 이전에 알려지지 않은 사실을 소개할 때 사용됩니다. 상황에 대한 청자의 이해를 바꿀 수 있는 정보를 드러낼 때 쓰이는 신호 표현입니다.","examples":["It turns out that we''re fighting one of the most evolutionarily conserved learning processes currently known in science,"],"segmentIndex":12},{"pattern":"What if + past tense (Hypothetical suggestion)","explanation":"The ''What if + past tense'' structure is used to propose a hypothetical scenario or suggestion. It softens the suggestion and makes it more tentative and inviting.","explanationKo":"''What if + 과거 시제'' 구조는 가상의 시나리오나 제안을 할 때 사용됩니다. 제안을 부드럽게 만들고 더 잠정적이고 초대하는 느낌을 줍니다.","examples":["What if instead of fighting our brains... we instead tapped into this natural reward-based learning process,","What if instead, we just got really curious about what was happening in our momentary experience?"],"segmentIndex":46},{"pattern":"Rhetorical questions for engagement","explanation":"Rhetorical questions are asked not to get an answer but to make the audience think. They are a common public speaking technique to engage listeners and transition between ideas.","explanationKo":"수사적 질문은 답변을 얻기 위해서가 아니라 청중이 생각하게 만들기 위해 사용됩니다. 청취자의 관심을 끌고 아이디어 간 전환을 하기 위한 일반적인 발표 기법입니다.","examples":["So why is it so hard to pay attention?","So what''s going on here?","Simple, right?","What does curiosity feel like?"],"segmentIndex":8},{"pattern":"Passive voice with ''is called''","explanation":"The passive construction ''is called'' is commonly used in academic and educational contexts to introduce or define technical terminology without focusing on who coined the term.","explanationKo":"''is called'' 수동태 구문은 학술적, 교육적 맥락에서 용어를 만든 사람에 초점을 두지 않고 전문 용어를 소개하거나 정의할 때 자주 사용됩니다.","examples":["This reward-based learning process is called positive and negative reinforcement","Called the default mode network were at play."],"segmentIndex":15},{"pattern":"Not... but (Contrast structure)","explanation":"This structure sets up a contrast between what is not the case and what actually is. It is used to correct assumptions or redirect the listener''s thinking.","explanationKo":"''not... but'' 구조는 사실이 아닌 것과 실제 사실 사이의 대조를 설정합니다. 가정을 수정하거나 청자의 생각을 전환하는 데 사용됩니다.","examples":["the posterior cingulate cortex is activated not necessarily by craving itself, but when we get caught up in it","This isn''t to say that, poof, magically we quit smoking, but over time..."],"segmentIndex":118},{"pattern":"Rather than + gerund (Contrasting alternatives)","explanation":"''Rather than'' followed by a gerund is used to present an alternative action, often contrasting a less desirable approach with a preferred one.","explanationKo":"''rather than + 동명사''는 대안적 행동을 제시할 때 사용되며, 덜 바람직한 접근법과 더 좋은 접근법을 대조합니다.","examples":["Rather than trying to make unpleasant cravings go away as quickly as possible.","rather than getting clobbered by this huge, scary craving that we choke on."],"segmentIndex":98},{"pattern":"Present perfect for relevance to now","explanation":"The present perfect tense (''have gone'', ''have tried'') connects past actions to the present situation, emphasizing that the effects or results are still relevant now.","explanationKo":"현재완료 시제(''have gone'', ''have tried'')는 과거의 행동을 현재 상황에 연결하여, 그 효과나 결과가 지금도 여전히 관련이 있음을 강조합니다.","examples":["We''ve gone from learning to survive to literally killing ourselves with these habits.","the majority of them had tried this before and failed,"],"segmentIndex":43},{"pattern":"In other words (Rephrasing signal)","explanation":"''In other words'' is a discourse marker used to restate or clarify a previous idea in simpler or different terms, helping the audience understand a complex concept.","explanationKo":"''In other words''는 이전 아이디어를 더 간단하거나 다른 용어로 재진술하거나 명확히 하기 위해 사용되는 담화 표지입니다. 복잡한 개념을 청중이 이해하도록 돕습니다.","examples":["In other words, when we get curious, we step out of our old fear-based reactive habit patterns,"],"segmentIndex":107},{"pattern":"Comparative structure with ''twice as... as''","explanation":"The ''twice as [adjective] as'' structure makes a precise comparison, indicating that one thing is double the degree of another. This is common in academic and research contexts.","explanationKo":"''twice as [형용사] as'' 구조는 정밀한 비교를 하며, 한 가지가 다른 것의 두 배임을 나타냅니다. 학술 및 연구 맥락에서 흔히 사용됩니다.","examples":["mindfulness training was twice as good as gold standard therapy at helping people quit smoking."],"segmentIndex":114},{"pattern":"Instead of + noun/gerund (Substitution)","explanation":"''Instead of'' introduces an alternative to something mentioned, often used to propose a different action or approach. It highlights the contrast between the rejected option and the chosen one.","explanationKo":"''Instead of''는 언급된 것에 대한 대안을 소개하며, 다른 행동이나 접근법을 제안할 때 자주 사용됩니다. 거부된 선택과 선택된 것 사이의 대조를 강조합니다.","examples":["Instead of this hunger signal coming from our stomach,","Instead of see text message, compulsively text back,"],"segmentIndex":31},{"pattern":"Conditional ''if'' for audience engagement","explanation":"Using ''if'' clauses directed at the audience creates a conditional scenario that listeners can personally relate to, making the content more relevant and persuasive.","explanationKo":"청중에게 향한 ''if'' 조건절은 청취자가 개인적으로 공감할 수 있는 조건적 시나리오를 만들어, 내용을 더 관련성 있고 설득력 있게 만듭니다.","examples":["So if you don''t smoke or stress eat, maybe the next time you feel this urge to check your email when you''re bored..."],"segmentIndex":132}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"reduction","originalText":"I''d sit","phonetic":"/aɪd sɪt/","koreanPhonetic":"아읻 싯","explanation":"''I would'' is contracted to ''I''d'', reducing two syllables to one. This is extremely common in fast natural speech.","explanationKo":"''I would''가 ''I''d''로 축약되어 두 음절이 한 음절로 줄어듭니다. 빠른 자연스러운 대화에서 매우 일반적입니다.","practiceGuide":"''아이''와 ''d'' 소리를 빠르게 연결하세요. ''would''의 ''woul'' 부분은 완전히 사라집니다.","segmentIndex":4},{"type":"linking","originalText":"turns out","phonetic":"/tɜːrnz‿aʊt/","koreanPhonetic":"턴자웃","explanation":"The final /z/ of ''turns'' links directly to the vowel /aʊ/ of ''out'', creating a smooth connection without any pause between the words.","explanationKo":"''turns''의 마지막 /z/ 소리가 ''out''의 모음 /aʊ/에 직접 연결되어 단어 사이에 쉼 없이 부드럽게 이어집니다.","practiceGuide":"''턴스''와 ''아웃''을 따로 발음하지 말고, ''턴자웃''처럼 하나로 연결해서 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":12},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"kind of","phonetic":"/kaɪndə/","koreanPhonetic":"카인다","explanation":"''Kind of'' is frequently reduced in casual speech to ''kinda'', where the ''of'' loses its vowel and final consonant, becoming just a schwa sound.","explanationKo":"''Kind of''는 일상적인 대화에서 ''kinda''로 자주 축약되며, ''of''의 모음과 마지막 자음이 사라지고 슈와 소리만 남습니다.","practiceGuide":"''카인드 오브''라고 하지 말고 ''카인다''로 빠르게 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":27},{"type":"linking","originalText":"pay attention","phonetic":"/peɪ‿əˈtenʃən/","koreanPhonetic":"페이어텐션","explanation":"The final diphthong /eɪ/ of ''pay'' glides smoothly into the initial schwa /ə/ of ''attention'', creating a seamless connection.","explanationKo":"''pay''의 마지막 이중모음 /eɪ/가 ''attention''의 첫 슈와 /ə/로 부드럽게 미끄러져 자연스럽게 연결됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''페이''와 ''어텐션''을 끊지 않고 ''페이어텐션''으로 한 호흡에 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":2},{"type":"elision","originalText":"want to","phonetic":"/wɒnə/","koreanPhonetic":"워나","explanation":"''Want to'' is commonly reduced to ''wanna'' in spoken English. The /t/ at the end of ''want'' and the /t/ at the beginning of ''to'' are both dropped.","explanationKo":"''Want to''는 구어체 영어에서 ''wanna''로 흔히 축약됩니다. ''want''의 끝 /t/와 ''to''의 시작 /t/가 모두 탈락합니다.","practiceGuide":"''원트 투''가 아니라 ''워나''로 빠르게 발음하세요. 중간의 ''t'' 소리를 모두 생략합니다.","segmentIndex":35},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"going to","phonetic":"/ˈɡɒnə/","koreanPhonetic":"거나","explanation":"''Going to'' is reduced to ''gonna'' in natural speech. The ''-ing'' ending and the word ''to'' merge into a simple ''-na'' sound.","explanationKo":"''Going to''는 자연스러운 대화에서 ''gonna''로 축약됩니다. ''-ing'' 어미와 ''to''가 합쳐져 단순한 ''-나'' 소리가 됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''고잉 투''가 아니라 ''거나''로 자연스럽게 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":80},{"type":"assimilation","originalText":"don''t you","phonetic":"/doʊntʃuː/","koreanPhonetic":"돈츄","explanation":"When /t/ at the end of ''don''t'' meets /j/ at the start of ''you'', they assimilate to produce /tʃ/ (a ''ch'' sound). This is called palatalization.","explanationKo":"''don''t''의 끝 /t/가 ''you''의 시작 /j/를 만나면 /tʃ/ (''ㅊ'' 소리)로 동화됩니다. 이것을 구개음화라고 합니다.","practiceGuide":"''돈트 유''가 아니라 ''돈츄''로 발음하세요. ''t''와 ''y''가 만나 ''ch'' 소리가 됩니다.","segmentIndex":28},{"type":"linking","originalText":"step out of","phonetic":"/stɛp‿aʊt‿əv/","koreanPhonetic":"스테파우터브","explanation":"Three words link together: the /p/ of ''step'' links to the /aʊ/ of ''out'', and the /t/ of ''out'' links to the /ə/ of ''of'', creating a fluid three-word chain.","explanationKo":"세 단어가 연결됩니다: ''step''의 /p/가 ''out''의 /aʊ/에, ''out''의 /t/가 ''of''의 /ə/에 연결되어 유동적인 세 단어 체인을 만듭니다.","practiceGuide":"세 단어를 끊지 않고 ''스테파우터브''처럼 하나로 이어서 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":108},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"you know","phonetic":"/jənoʊ/","koreanPhonetic":"야노","explanation":"''You know'' is heavily reduced in casual speech. ''You'' becomes /jə/ and links directly to ''know'', functioning as a filler phrase.","explanationKo":"''You know''는 일상적인 대화에서 크게 축약됩니다. ''You''가 /jə/가 되고 ''know''에 직접 연결되어, 삽입어 역할을 합니다.","practiceGuide":"''유 노우''가 아니라 ''야노''처럼 빠르게 발음하세요. 화자가 다음 말을 이을 때 흔히 쓰는 표현입니다.","segmentIndex":27},{"type":"linking","originalText":"feel good","phonetic":"/fiːl‿ɡʊd/","koreanPhonetic":"필굳","explanation":"The /l/ at the end of ''feel'' links smoothly to the /ɡ/ at the start of ''good''. The lateral /l/ flows into the velar /ɡ/ without any break.","explanationKo":"''feel''의 마지막 /l/이 ''good''의 시작 /ɡ/에 부드럽게 연결됩니다. 설측음 /l/이 연구개음 /ɡ/로 끊김 없이 이어집니다.","practiceGuide":"''필''과 ''굳''을 따로 말하지 말고, 혀를 윗잇몸에 댄 채 바로 ''g'' 소리로 이어가세요.","segmentIndex":23},{"type":"elision","originalText":"just by being","phonetic":"/dʒʌs baɪ ˈbiːɪŋ/","koreanPhonetic":"저스 바이 비잉","explanation":"The final /t/ in ''just'' is often dropped (elided) before the consonant /b/ in ''by''. This is a common feature of natural English speech to maintain flow.","explanationKo":"''just''의 마지막 /t/가 ''by''의 자음 /b/ 앞에서 종종 탈락(생략)됩니다. 자연스러운 영어 발화에서 흐름을 유지하기 위한 일반적인 특징입니다.","practiceGuide":"''저스트 바이''가 아니라 ''저스 바이''로 발음하세요. ''t'' 소리를 생략하면 더 자연스럽습니다.","segmentIndex":121},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"let go of","phonetic":"/lɛt ɡoʊ‿əv/","koreanPhonetic":"렛고어브","explanation":"In this common phrase, ''of'' is reduced from /ɒv/ to just a schwa /əv/ or even /ə/. The phrase flows as a single unit.","explanationKo":"이 흔한 구문에서 ''of''는 /ɒv/에서 슈와 /əv/ 또는 /ə/로 축약됩니다. 이 구문은 하나의 단위처럼 흘러갑니다.","practiceGuide":"''렛 고 오브''가 아니라 ''렛고어브''처럼 빠르게 연결하여 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":93},{"type":"assimilation","originalText":"did you","phonetic":"/dɪdʒuː/","koreanPhonetic":"디쥬","explanation":"The /d/ at the end of ''did'' combines with the /j/ at the start of ''you'' to produce /dʒ/ (a ''j'' sound). This is another example of palatalization.","explanationKo":"''did''의 끝 /d/가 ''you''의 시작 /j/와 결합하여 /dʒ/ (''ㅈ'' 소리)를 만듭니다. 이것은 구개음화의 또 다른 예입니다.","practiceGuide":"''디드 유''가 아니라 ''디쥬''로 발음하세요. ''And what did they notice?'' 문장에서 연습해보세요.","segmentIndex":61},{"type":"linking","originalText":"smoke to be cool","phonetic":"/smoʊk tə bi kuːl/","koreanPhonetic":"스모욱 터 비 쿨","explanation":"The phrase links together with ''to'' reduced to /tə/. The continuous flow of consonants and vowels creates a rhythmic, connected speech pattern.","explanationKo":"''to''가 /tə/로 축약되면서 구문이 연결됩니다. 자음과 모음의 연속적인 흐름이 리듬감 있고 연결된 말하기 패턴을 만듭니다.","practiceGuide":"''투 비''가 아니라 ''터 비''로 축약하여, 전체 구문을 리듬감 있게 이어서 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":37},{"type":"reduction","originalText":"sort of","phonetic":"/ˈsɔːrtə/","koreanPhonetic":"소러","explanation":"Similar to ''kind of'', ''sort of'' reduces to ''sorta'' in casual speech. The ''of'' is reduced to just a schwa.","explanationKo":"''kind of''와 마찬가지로 ''sort of''도 일상적인 대화에서 ''sorta''로 축약됩니다. ''of''가 슈와로만 축약됩니다.","practiceGuide":"''소트 오브''가 아니라 ''소러''처럼 빠르게 발음하세요.","segmentIndex":57}]'::jsonb, '{"sections":[{"section":"Personal Hook: Meditation Struggles","startSegment":0,"endSegment":8,"summary":"The speaker opens with a personal anecdote about his early struggles with meditation, describing how difficult it was to simply pay attention to his breath despite the simplicity of the instructions.","summaryKo":"연사는 명상 초기의 개인적인 어려움에 대한 일화로 시작합니다. 지시 사항이 단순했음에도 호흡에 집중하는 것이 얼마나 어려웠는지 설명합니다.","keyPoints":["명상의 지시 사항은 간단했지만 실행은 극도로 어려웠다","중요한 무언가를 놓치고 있었다는 깨달음"]},{"section":"The Science: Reward-Based Learning","startSegment":9,"endSegment":25,"summary":"The speaker explains the evolutionarily conserved reward-based learning process: trigger, behavior, reward. He uses the simple example of food to illustrate how our brains learn through positive and negative reinforcement.","summaryKo":"연사는 진화적으로 보존된 보상 기반 학습 과정인 자극-행동-보상을 설명합니다. 음식의 간단한 예시를 사용하여 뇌가 긍정적, 부정적 강화를 통해 학습하는 방법을 보여줍니다.","keyPoints":["진화적으로 가장 오래 보존된 학습 과정 중 하나","자극(trigger) - 행동(behavior) - 보상(reward)의 루프","맥락 의존적 기억의 형성"]},{"section":"How Habits Form: From Food to Smoking","startSegment":26,"endSegment":45,"summary":"The speaker extends the reward-based learning model to explain how emotional eating and smoking develop. The same trigger-behavior-reward loop gets hijacked, going from survival to self-destructive habits.","summaryKo":"연사는 보상 기반 학습 모델을 확장하여 감정적 폭식과 흡연이 어떻게 발전하는지 설명합니다. 같은 자극-행동-보상 루프가 생존에서 자기 파괴적 습관으로 변질됩니다.","keyPoints":["감정적 자극이 배고픔 자극을 대체할 수 있다","사회적 압력이 흡연 같은 습관을 유발한다","비만과 흡연은 세계 주요 예방 가능 사망 원인"]},{"section":"The Twist: Curiosity as an Alternative","startSegment":46,"endSegment":65,"summary":"The speaker introduces the key twist: instead of fighting cravings, use curiosity. He describes a mindfulness-based smoking cessation study where participants were told to smoke but be curious about it, leading to a visceral aversion.","summaryKo":"연사는 핵심적인 변형을 소개합니다: 갈망과 싸우는 대신 호기심을 활용하라는 것입니다. 참가자들에게 담배를 피우되 호기심을 갖도록 한 마음챙김 기반 금연 연구를 설명합니다.","keyPoints":["뇌와 싸우는 대신 자연적 학습 과정을 활용","호기심이 행동 변화의 핵심 도구","마음챙김하며 흡연했더니 본능적 혐오감을 경험"]},{"section":"Knowledge vs. Wisdom: Disenchantment","startSegment":66,"endSegment":86,"summary":"The speaker distinguishes between knowing something intellectually and knowing it viscerally. He explains the role of the prefrontal cortex in cognitive control and why it fails under stress, making visceral disenchantment more effective.","summaryKo":"연사는 지적으로 아는 것과 본능적으로 아는 것의 차이를 구분합니다. 인지적 통제에서 전전두엽 피질의 역할과 스트레스 하에서 실패하는 이유를 설명하며, 본능적 환멸이 더 효과적인 이유를 밝힙니다.","keyPoints":["지식에서 지혜로의 전환이 행동 변화의 핵심","전전두엽 피질은 스트레스 시 가장 먼저 작동을 멈춤","환멸은 더 깊은 수준의 이해를 가능하게 함"]},{"section":"Mindfulness in Practice: Curiosity and Letting Go","startSegment":87,"endSegment":111,"summary":"The speaker explains that mindfulness is about clearly seeing what we get from our behaviors, becoming disenchanted, and naturally letting go. Curiosity is naturally rewarding and breaks cravings into manageable body sensations.","summaryKo":"연사는 마음챙김이 행동에서 무엇을 얻는지 명확하게 보고, 환멸을 느끼며, 자연스럽게 놓아버리는 것이라고 설명합니다. 호기심은 자연스럽게 보상이 되며 갈망을 관리 가능한 신체 감각으로 분해합니다.","keyPoints":["마음챙김은 경험을 명확히 보는 것","호기심 자체가 자연스러운 보상","갈망은 관리 가능한 신체 감각의 조합","두려움 기반 반응 패턴에서 존재로의 전환"]},{"section":"Scientific Evidence and Brain Research","startSegment":112,"endSegment":126,"summary":"The speaker presents research evidence: mindfulness training was twice as effective as standard therapy for smoking cessation. He discusses brain imaging findings about the default mode network and posterior cingulate cortex, and mentions app-based programs.","summaryKo":"연사는 연구 증거를 제시합니다: 마음챙김 훈련이 금연에서 표준 치료법보다 두 배 효과적이었습니다. 디폴트 모드 네트워크와 후대상피질에 대한 뇌 영상 연구 결과를 논의하고, 앱 기반 프로그램을 언급합니다.","keyPoints":["마음챙김 훈련이 표준 치료법 대비 금연 효과 2배","디폴트 모드 네트워크의 후대상피질이 갈망에 빠질 때 활성화","놓아버리면 해당 뇌 영역이 조용해짐","앱과 온라인 기반 프로그램 개발 중"]},{"section":"Call to Action: Apply It to Your Life","startSegment":127,"endSegment":143,"summary":"The speaker broadens the application beyond smoking to everyday habits like checking email or texting while driving. He urges the audience to tap into their natural curiosity, notice urges, and feel the joy of letting go.","summaryKo":"연사는 적용 범위를 흡연 너머 이메일 확인이나 운전 중 문자 같은 일상 습관으로 넓힙니다. 청중에게 자연스러운 호기심을 활용하고, 충동을 알아차리며, 놓아버리는 기쁨을 느끼라고 촉구합니다.","keyPoints":["맥락 의존적 기억을 활용한 적시 개입","일상적 습관에도 같은 원리 적용 가능","충동 알아차리기 - 호기심 갖기 - 놓아버리는 기쁨 - 반복"]}],"signalExpressions":[{"expression":"When I was first learning to meditate","type":"hook","segmentIndex":1,"role":"개인적 일화로 시작하여 청중의 관심을 끄는 도입부"},{"expression":"So why is it so hard to pay attention?","type":"transition","segmentIndex":8,"role":"수사적 질문으로 개인 경험에서 과학적 설명으로 전환"},{"expression":"So what''s going on here?","type":"transition","segmentIndex":11,"role":"청중의 공감을 이끌어낸 후 핵심 설명으로 전환하는 질문"},{"expression":"It turns out that","type":"transition","segmentIndex":12,"role":"놀라운 사실을 소개하며 본론으로 진입하는 신호"},{"expression":"And basically goes like this","type":"transition","segmentIndex":16,"role":"복잡한 개념을 단순화하여 설명하겠다는 신호"},{"expression":"So back to my breath","type":"transition","segmentIndex":45,"role":"도입부의 명상 이야기로 돌아가 해결책 제시로 전환"},{"expression":"What if instead","type":"transition","segmentIndex":49,"role":"핵심 해결책을 가설 형태로 제안하는 전환점"},{"expression":"I''ll give you an example","type":"transition","segmentIndex":51,"role":"추상적 개념에서 구체적 사례로 전환"},{"expression":"In other words","type":"transition","segmentIndex":107,"role":"핵심 메시지를 다른 표현으로 재진술하여 이해를 도움"},{"expression":"So it actually works","type":"transition","segmentIndex":115,"role":"이론에서 실증적 증거로의 전환을 강조"},{"expression":"So if you don''t smoke or stress eat","type":"transition","segmentIndex":132,"role":"연구 결과에서 청중의 실생활 적용으로 전환"},{"expression":"Instead of see text message, compulsively text back, feel a little bit better, notice the urge, get curious, feel the joy of letting go, and repeat.","type":"conclusion","segmentIndex":140,"role":"새로운 습관 루프를 제시하며 강연의 핵심 메시지를 요약"},{"expression":"Thank you.","type":"conclusion","segmentIndex":143,"role":"강연의 마무리 인사"}]}'::jsonb
)
